Rapidly Progressive Cumulative Incidence Of Coronavirus (COVID-19) in the European Union/European Economic Area and the United Kingdom, 1 January to 15 March 2020
Cumulative incidence of Corona Virovirus (COVID-19) cases shows similar trends in countries of the European Union/European Economic Area and in the United Kingdom, confirming that, although it is at a different stage depending on the country, COVID-19 pandemic is advancing rapidly in all countries.
Drawing on Italy's experience, countries, hospitals and ICUs should prepare more for an ontach outbreak of COVID-19 patients who will need intensive care.
As of December 31, 2019, a number of cases of uncharted etiology has been reported in Wuhan, Hubei Province, China.
On January 9, 2020, China’s Centers for Disease Control and Prevention reported the causative agent as being a novel coronavirus, now referred to as Coronavirus of Severe Acute Respiratory Syndrome (SARS-CoV-2.
Since then disease resulting from SARS-CoV-2 infection has been called Coronavirus (COVID-19).
Evidence to date is that 80% of people with COVID-19 develop a mild form of the disease, i.e., an infection of the respiratory tract with or without pneumonia, and most of them recover.
In about 14 percent of cases, COVID-19 progresses to a more serious disease, requiring hospitalization, while the 6% of remaining cases progress to a critical disease, requiring intensive treatment.
Mortality in hospitalized patients due to COVID-19 is 4%.
In this study, we evaluated trends in the cumulative incidence of COVID-19 in each European Union/European Economic Space (EU/EEA) and in the United Kingdom and compared them with those of Hubei Province in China.
We have also compared the number of COVID-19 cases in the EU/EEA countries and the United Kingdom with Italy between January 31st and March 15, 2020.
COVID-19 cases in EU/EEA countries and the United Kingdom
After China, COVID-19 spread geographically, and the dynamics of COVID--19 pandemic in the rest of the world currently follows that country.
On March 11, 2020, Director-in-Chi--Declaw of the World Health Organization (WHO) declared COVID-19 a pandemic.
The March 5 edition of Eurosurveillance 2020, Spiteri et al. reported the first confirmed COVID-19 cases in Europe, in accordance with WHO’s case-definition.
In the EU/EEO, the first three confirmed cases were reported by France on January 24, 2020 in people returning from Wuhan in Hubei Province, China.
On March 15, 2020, COVID-19 cases had already been detected in all 30 EU/EEA countries and in the United Kingdom between December 31, 2019 and date mentioned above, 39,768 cases and 1,727 have been reported, with 17,750 cases and 1,441 deaths in Italy alone.
Acquisition of Cumulative Figures and Cumulative Injuries of COVID-19
In the European Center for Disease Prevention and Control (ECDC), the number of reported COVID-19 cases in each country in the world, obtained only from official sources, such as the Ministry of Health of the countries, national and regional health authorities and the WHO, is updated every day at 8 a.m.
These data have been used to evaluate COVID-19 trends in the EU/EEA and the United Kingdom, and to compare them to those of Italy.
As an approximation of the prevalence of COVID-19 active cases, we calculate the cumulative truncational incidence of 14-sea days of COVID-19 cases, taking into account the natural evolution of COVID-19 in each EU/EEA country and in the United Kingdom during the period from January 1 to March 15, 2020.
We will also present the cumulative number of notified cases of each country by March 15, 2020 at 8 a.m., and complied with that of Italy from 31 January to March 15, 2020.
COVID-19 Trends in EU/EEE Countries and the United Kingdom
Trends in cumulating truncated 14-day COVID-19 in EU/EEA countries and in the United Kingdom in general have followed Hubei Province (Figure 1).
Overall for the EU/EEA and the United Kingdom, COVID-19’s cumulative incidence began to increase around February 21 and increased significantly thereafter around February 28, 2020 (complementary material).
This happened mainly due to the rapid increase in reported cases in Italy, but all other EU/EEA countries and the United Kingdom have shown similar upward trends in cumulative COVID-19 (complementary material).
Figure 2 shows the cumulative COVID-19 cases in EU/EEA countries and in the United Kingdom compared to Italy for the period from January 31 to March 15, 2020.
He points out that by March 15 at 8 a.m. other EU/EEA countries and the United Kingdom had already reported a total number of cases equating to Italy 3 weeks or less.
Our findings indicate that the number of COVID-19-reported cases is increasing rapidly in the EU/EEA and the United Kingdom.
The trends observed in the cumulative incidence of COVID-19 suggest that the pandemic is progressing at a similar speed in all countries.
This occurs even though the countries are at different stages, the variations in the actions of national public health systems, and possibly different definitions of cases in the countries and different protocols for the selection of patients that should be tested for COVID-19 confirmation, including testing for updating.
At the beginning of March of 2020, physicians in the affected regions of Italy described a situation in which 10% of COVID-19 patients needed intensive treatment, and the media reported that hospitals and intensive treatment units in these regions had already reached their maximum capacity.
Data on hospitalization for cases of COVID-19 in a hospital and/or an ICU are currently available at EU/EEA for only 6% and 19% of cases, respectively (data not shown).
It should, however, be collected in a systematic way to supplement current surveillance data with a focus on the number of reported cases and the death toll.
A study conducted between 2010-2011 showed a huge variation in the availability of ICU beds and semi-intensive units in Europe, ranging from 29.2 beds in Germany to 4.2 beds in Portugal for every 100 thousand people.
This means that the countries may have more or less resources than Italy (12,5 ICU beds and semi-intensive treatment for a population of 100,000 between 2010 and 2011.
Scenarios related to the saturation saturation of health system capacity, with estimates for each EU/EEA country and for the United Kingdom the prevalence of COVID-19 hospitalisation cases associated with a greater risk of 90% of intensive care-milk bed capacity are provided in the sixth update of ECDC Rapid Risk Assessment on COVID-19.
Given that cases have so far been focused in certain regions of EU/EEA countries and the United Kingdom, hospitals and intensive care units generally serve a defined regional population, information on cases and intensive care beds should preferably be made available in the Nomenclature of Territorial Units for Statistical Purposes 2 (NUTS-2).
The example of Italy and trends in other countries show that the COVID-19 pandemic is progressing quickly in the EU/EEA and the United Kingdom.
Countries, hospitals and ICUs should therefore be prepared for a scenario of sustained community transmission of SARS-CoV-2 and for an increase in the number of COVID-19 patients who will require medical care, especially intensive care, such as the one that occurs in the affected regions of Italy.
As pointed out in ECDC’s Rapid Risk Assessment, a quick, proactive and comprehensive approach is essential to delay the spread of SARS-CoV-CoV–2, with a shift from the containment approach to a mitigation approach, as the anticipated rapid increase in the number of cases may not offer sufficient time for decision-makers and hospitals to understand, accept, and adapt their actions appropriately if not implemented.
The rapid risk assessment will also list public health measures to mitigate the impact of an epidemic.
There is a small window of opportunity during which countries have the possibility of intensifying their control initiatives in order to decrease the spread of SARS-CoV-2 and reduce pressure on the health system.
If they fail in these initiatives, it is likely that the health systems of other UE/EEA countries will have an outbreak of patients who will need intensive care in the coming days or weeks.
Coronavirus disease of 2019 (COVID-19), caused by Coronavirus Disease 2 (SARS-CoV-2) of Severe Acute Respiratory Respiratory Syndrome (SARS), has killed more than 3,000 people and infected more than 80,000 in China and the rest of the world, resulting in a catastrophe for the population.
Similar to its homolog-occurate virus SARS-CoV, which caused SARS in thousands of people in 2003, SARS-CoV-2 could also be transmitted from the bats and causes similar symptoms through a similar mechanism.
COVID-19 has a lower severity and mortality than SARS is much more transmissive and affects older people more than young people and more men.
In response to the rapidly increasing number of publications about the new disease, this article seeks to provide a current and comprehensive evaluation of the research object, which develops quickly.
We will deal the basic principles of epidemiology, etiology, virology, diagnosis, treatment, prognosis and disease prevention.
While many questions still require answers, we hope this study will help understand and eradicate this hostile disease.
On January 25, 2020, the Spring Film Festival has become a unique and unforgettable memorandandum for all Chinese who have been required to be confined throughout and for several subsequent weeks due to an epidemic of a new viral disease.
The virus bears great resemblance to coronavirus (CoV), which caused an epidemic of severe acute respiratory syndrome (SARS) in 2003; therefore it has been named SARS-CoV-2 by the World Health Organization (WHO) on February 11, 2020, and its disease has been called CoV-19 (COVID-19).
Epidemic began in Wuhan, China, and spread rapidly throughout the country and nearly 50 other countries around the world.
By March 2, 2020, the virus had resulted in over 8,800 confirmed COVID-19 cases with more than 4,40,000 patients recovered and over 4,000 dead patients.
WHO warns that COVID--19 is the “number one public enemy”, and is potentially more powerful than terrorism.
According to PubMed (https:/www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/), in less than two months, more than 200 articles have been published on COVID-19, including its virology, epidemiology, etiology, diagnosis, and treatment since the first report on Jan. 7, 2020, which determined the sequencing of the isolated virus of several patients.
“This article aims to summarise the progress of research in this new area of rapid development.
Whenever it is relevant, we will compare COVID-19 to SARS and another disease caused by CoV, Middle Eastern Respiratory Syndrome
We’ll also discuss what we’ve learned so far about the prevention and prognosis of the disease, as well as other pressing issues.
CoVs are traditionally considered non-lethal pathogen to humans, mainly causing about 15 percent of common colds.
However, in this century, we have twice encountered highly pathogenic human CoV, which are SARS-CoV and MERS-CoV, causing an epidemic originating in China in 2003 and in Saudi Arabia in 2012, respectively, and which soon spread to several other countries with frightening morbidity and mortality.
Therefore current COVID-19 is the third CoV-adjectory epidemic in human documented history.
As shown at Fig.1,1, a portion of cases of pneumonia with unknown origins was initially reported in Wuhan on December 31, 2019, to the National Commission of Health of China.
Seven days later the sequencing CoV was published.
On January 15, 2020, Wuhan's first fatal case was reported.
In the meantime, the epidemic spread rapidly to neighboring cities, provinces and countries.
On January 20, contagion of health professionals was communicated, suggesting that the transmission between people was possible.
On January 23, Wuhan's city was confined, with all its public transport disrupted.
On January 24, the first clinical study on the disease reported that out of 41 patients with confirmed cases only 21 had direct contact with Wuhan's seafood market, which was considered to be the on-set site of contagion by an undischarted animal source.
On January 30, the WHO declared the epidemic a global health emergency.
To date with this report, the disease has spread throughout China and nearly 50 other countries around the world (Figs. 2).
As the situation worsens rapidly, the size and final gravity of the epidemic should still be determined.
On February 11, 2020, a study of several centers with 8,866 patients, including 4,021 patients confirmed with COVID-19, presented a more up-to-date illustration of the Epidemic, as follows (https:/mp.weixin.qq.com/s/UlBi-HX-rHPX1qHA2bhdA).
SARS-CoV-2 has infected people of all ages, especially in the age group of 30-65 years.
Almost half (47.7 percent) of those infected were over the age of more than 50, some were under the age of 20, and only 14 infected were under the age of 10.
SARS-CoV-2 infected more men (0.31/100,000) than women (0.27/100,000).
COVID-19 has spread in groups, especially in Hubei and its surroundings.
COVID-19 has received an average of five (2 to 9) days for diagnosis based on the onset of symptoms.
The mean incubation period was 4.8 3.6 (2.0 to 7.2) days.
The mean time of death based on the onset of symptoms was 9.5 (4.5 (+7.8 to 13) days.
The basic reproduction number (R0) was 3.77 (95% CI: 3.51 to 4.05)), and adjusted R0 was 2.23 to 4.82.
Infective numbers of people grew exponentially before January 23, 2020, corresponding to massive transportation time ahead of the Spring Festival in China.
Mortality of patients with confirmed cases was 1.44% (95% CI: 1.10% to 1.86%), and the adjusted-morrithment of all patients was 3.06% (95% CI: 2.02% to 4.99%).
The three major risk factors for COVID-19 are gender (maculino), (befelderly aged 60) and severe pneumonia.
CoVs are a sub-family of large and encapsulated viruses that contain a single RNA strand with certain meaning.
They can be broken down into four genera: alpha, beta, squat and delta, of which alphacoronavirus and beta-coronavirus are known to infect humans.
Glycoproteine spike(S) of the envelope is joined to its agiotensin-conververdulain 2 (CEA) converting cell receptors angiotensin 2 (ECA2) and dipeptidil peptidase 4 (DPP4) for SARS-CoV and MERS-CoV, respectively, membrane fusion occurs.
Viral RNA genome is released in the cytoplasm; after replication of the viral genome, genomic RNA accompanied by encapsulated glycoProteins and Nucleocapsid proteins forms vesicle-containing vesicles, which join the plasma membrane and liberate the virus.
The first Sequencing of SARS-CoV-2 was reported on January 10, 2020.
SARS-CoV-CoV-2 has been found to be a new type of betacoronavirus with over 90.98% genetic identity among 10 sequenced samples collected from the same original site of the outbreak, Huanan Sea Fruit Market in Wuhan.
SARS-CoV-CoV-2 is genetically similar to SARS-CoV as MERS-CoV.
Through transmission electron microscolocity, SARS-CoV-2 particles were discovered in ultra-threnheres sections of human airway epithelium.
It was found that human ECA2 is a receiver for SARS-CoV as well as SARS-CoV.
However, SARS-CoV-2 S-Probine binds human protein ECA2 weaker than SARS-CoV protein, which coincides with SARS-CoV-2 causes less severe infection in patients than SARS-CoV.
SARS-CoV-CoV-2 can also form a new short-protein encoded by orf3b and a besplified protein encoded by orf8.
orf3b SARS-CoV-CoV-2 may perform a function in viral pathogenicity and inhibit NMI expression; however, orf8 does not contain any known functional domain or motif.
On February 18, 2020 Zhou, et al. reported the cryo-EM structure of total-length human ACA at a resolution of 2.9 in a complex with the Amehemia transporter B0AT1.
It turned out that the complex, which there were open and closed conformations, was grouped together as a dimer, and the ECA2-B0AT1 complex can join two S-proteins, which provides evidence for reconnaissance and CoV Infection.
B0AT1 may become therapeutic target for screening medications to suppress SARS-CoV-2 infection.
The host of origin and the inter-intermediate
It is known that both SARS-CoV and MERS-CoV originated from the bats and were transmitted to humans through civets and camels, respectively.
Through a phylogenetic comparison of SARS-CoV-CoV-2 with other CoVs, the bats were considered the native hosts of SARS-CoV-2, since the new virus is 96% identical to two COVs of bats similar to SARS called bat-SL-CoVZX45 and bat-SL-CoVZX21.
However, the intermediary host that helped the virus to cross species barriers and infect humans remains undiscovered, and the transmission route has yet to be discovered.
Ji, et al., suggests that snakes transport the virus from the bats to humans, which involved a homologous recombination in S protein.
According to a study, researchers in Guangzhou, China, have suggested that pangolins, elongated muzzle mammals that feed on ants and are frequently used in traditional Chinese medicine, are the potential interweamber hosts of SARS-CoV-2, relying on genetic homology of 99% between a CoV discovered in pangolins and SARS-CoV-2.
However, a difference of 1 percent in two genomes is a big difference, so concoclusive results for concrete evidence are being awaited (Fig.
Physico-chemistry Properties of SARS-CoV-Computer are, for the most part, undisclostated.
SARS-CoV and MERS-CoV can survive in-vited for 48 hours in a dry environment and for up to 5 days below 20 ?C with humidity from 40% to 50%.
SARS-CoV-2 has similar properties.
SARS-CoV-2 has been reported to be sensitive to ultra-violet rays and heat of 56oC for 30 minutes; ether, Ethanobin-Foltility, chlorine disinfectant with chloracetic, chloretic acid, chloroformer and other organic solvents, but not chlorhexidine, are efficient in deactivating the virus.
In general, the entire human population does not have SARS–CoV-2 immunity, and is therefore susceptible to the new virus.
Currently, no in-detailed studies have been reported regarding the immunological Response to SARS-CoV-CoV-2.
Therefore, we can only look at previous studies on other CoVs, especially on SARS-CoV and MERS-CoV (Fig. (Fig.4).
Overall, after a virus invades the host, it is first recognized by the host’s innate immune system through pattern recognition receivers (PRRs), including lectin-type C-type receptor (TLR), NOD-receiving (NLR).
Through different routes, the virus induces the expression of inflammatory factors, maturation of dendritic cells and synthesis of type I interpherons (IFNs), which limit the propagation of the virus and accelerate the phagocytosis of macrofaphages of viral antigens.
However, SARS-CoV Protein N may help the virus escape immune responses.
In a short time, the adaptive immune response joins the fight against the virus.
Lymphocytes including CD4+ and CD8+ T-cells play an important role in defense.
CD4+ T-cells stimulate B-cells to produce virus-specific antibodies and CD8+ T-cells directly culmously kill virus-infected cells.
Collaborative T-cells produce pro-inflammatory cytokines to help protect cells.
However, CoV may inhibit T-cell functions by inducing apoptosis of T-secular.
Moorical immunity, including supplements such as C3a and C5a anti-antibodies, is also essential for combating viral infection.
For example, antibodies isolated from a recovered patient neutralized MERS-CoV.
On the other hand, an exacerbated reaction of the immune system generates a large number of local free radicals that can cause serious damage to the lungs and other organs and, at worst, multiple organ failure, and even death.
Infection with SARS-CoV-CoV-2, characterized by the onset of symptoms in a group of people, more affects the elderly with comorbidities and pregnant women.
It’s common for people who are exposed to a large number of viruses whose immune system is compromised have a greater chance of being infected than other people.
Exempulsible mean incubation period of SARS-CoV-2 is from 1 to 14 days and, in most cases, from 3 to 7 days, based on a study of the first 425 cases in Wuhan.
However, a study of 1,099 cases showed that the incubation period was for 3 days on average and ranged from 0 to 24 days.
A more recent study as described above demonstrates that the incubation was 4.8 3.4 (2.0 to 7.2) days, based on demography of 8,866 cases.
It is essential that the health authorities adjust the effective quarantine time based on the most accurate in incubation, thus preventing infected people who do not have symptoms from transmitting the virus to other people.
As common practice, individuals who are exposed to or infected by the virus often need to be quarantined for for 14 days.
Should quarantine period be extended for 24 days?
Fever is usually the initial and primary symptom of COVID--19, which may come on its own or accompanied by other symptoms, such as dry cough, shortness of breath, muscle soreness, dizziness, sore-snoring, hoisting, diarrhoea, nausea and vomit.
Some had dyspnoia and/or hypoxemia one week after the onset of symptoms of the disease.
In severe cases, the patients rapidly evolved into an acute respiratory syndrome, septic shock, metabolic acidosis, and coagulopathy.
Patients with respiratory fever and/or symptoms and acute adolescent fever, even with no abnormalities at the diagnostic imaging of the lungs, should undergo screening for the virus in order to receive an early diagnosis.
A demographic study at the end of December of 2019 showed that percentages of symptoms were 98% to fever, 76% for dry cough, 55% for dyspnea and 3% for diarrhea; 8% of patients needed ventilatory support.
Sm-positive results have been reported in two recent studies of a family grouping and of a grouping with transmission of an asymptomatic individual.
In comparison, one demographic study in 2012 showed MERS-CoV patients also had fever (98%), dry cough (47%), and dyspnoea (55%).
However, 80% of the patients needed ventilatory support, much more so than COVID--19 patients, which consists of the higher MERS mortality compared to COVID-19.
Diarrhea (26%) and sore-demjoins (21%) were also observed in MERS patients.
It was demonstrated that fever (99% to 100%), dry cough (29% to 75%), dyspnea (40% to 42%), diarrrhoea (20% to 25%), and sore-threw pain (13% to 25%) were the main symptoms and ventilatory support was necessary for approximately 14% to 20% of patients.
Until February 14, COVID--19 mortality was 2 per 2 per cent, when world-confirmed cases reached 6,5,576.
Compared, SARS mortality until November 2002 was 10% of 8,096 confirmed cases.
According to MERS, based on a demographic study from June 2012, mortality was 37% of 2,494 confirmed cases.
A more recent study reported that SARS-CoV-SARS-CoV--2 basic reproductive number was up to 6.47 with a confidence interval (CI) of 95% 5.71 to 7.23, while SARS-CoV R0 ranged from 2–4.
A SARS-CoV-2 comparison with MERS-CoV and SARS-CoV in relation to its symptoms, mortality and R0 is presented in the Table 1.
The above figures suggest SARS-CoV-2 has a greater propagation capacity than MERS-CoV and SARS-CoV, but is less lethal than the latter.
Therefore, it is a much greater challenge to control the SARS-CoV-2 epidemic than MERS-CoV and SARS-CoV.
The onset of symptoms in a group of people usually occurs in the same family or in the same agglomeration or vehicle, such as a cruise ship.
Patients often have a history of travel or residence in Wuhan or other affected areas, or contact with individuals or patients infected in the two weeks prior to the onset of symptoms.
However, it has been reported that people can carry the virus for more than two weeks without presenting symptoms, and cured patients who were discharged from hospitals can contract the virus again, which generates an alarm to prolong quarantine.
The patients have a normal or reduced number of peripheral blood cells (mainly lymphocytes) at an early stage.
For example, lymphopenia with white blood cell count below 4 x 109/L including mphocyte counts less than 1 x 109/L, and elevated aspartate aminotransferase and viraemia levels were found in 1,099 COVID-19 patients.
Enzyme and myoglobin levels in muscles and the liver were increased in the blood of some patients, and C-reactive protein and erythrocyte sedimentation were increased in the blood of the majority of patients.
In patients with severe cases, D-dimeral, a product of fibril degradation present in the blood, was high and the lymphocyte count showed a progressive reduction.
Absorptities on chest radiography are present in most patients with COVID--19 and have bilateral irregular shades or ground-glass opacity in the lungs.
The patients generally developed an atypopic pneumonia, acute damage to the Lung and Acquired Respiratory Disglomese Syndrome (AKDPR).
When ARDS, unintraparous inflammation occurs, accumulation of fluid and progressive fibrosis severely imprute the exchange of gases.
Dysmuocytes of I-type and type-II reduces surfactant level and increases surface tension, thereby reducing the ability of the lungs to expand and increasing the risk of pulmonary collapse.
Therefore, the more severe chest radiography results usually correspond to the most severe form of the disease.
On February 18, 2020, the first pathological analysis of COVID-19 demonstrated squashing of pneumotics, the formation of the hyaline membrane, the infiltration of interstitial lymphocyte and synchospital cells in the lungs of a patient who died of the disease, consistent with the pathology of viral infection and ARDS, and similar to the pathology of SARS and MERS patients.
Sensition of SARS-CoV-2 RNA by polymerase-reverse transcriptase/reversal transcriptase (RT-PCR) RNA has been used as the main criterion for diagnosING COVID-19.
However, due to the high rate of false-negative, which can accelerate the epidemic, clinical manifestations have begun to be used for the diagnosis (which was not derived solely from RT-PCR) in China on February 13, 2020.
A similar situation also occurred with SARS diagnosis.
Therefore, a combination of disease history, clinical manifestations, laboratory tests and radiological outcomes is essential and indispensable to arrive at an effective diagnosis.
On Feb. 14, 2020, Feng Zhang described a protocol of use of SHERLOCK technique, based on CRISPR to detect SARS-CoV-CoV-2, which detects synthetic RNA snippets of SARS-CoV-CoV-2 at 20 x 10–18 mol/L at 200 x 10–18 mol/L (100-copies per micro-input microliter) using a measuring rod in less than an hour.
We expect the new technique to dramatically increase the sensitivity and convenience if verified on clinical samples.
Due due to a lack of experience with the new CoV, physicians can provide, to the best of their capacity, palliative treatment for COVID-19 patients, at the same time attempting a variety of therapies that have been used or proposed previously for the treatment of other CoVs such as SARS-CoV and MERS-CoV and other viral diseases (Table).
These therapies include current and potential anti-vector treatments, immunosuppressants, steroids, cured patient plasma, Chinese medicine and psychological support.
Plasma of patients who have recovered has been suggested as a treatment.
Drug companies are racing against the weather to develop anti-virus antibodies and vaccines.
SARS-CoV-2 primarily attacks the lungs at the beginning, and probably also attacks, to a lesser degree, other organs with ECA expression such as the dynager system and the kidney.
Respiratory dysfunction and organ failure are the main threats to patients and the main causes of death.
Respiratory support is therefore essential for relievoking symptoms and saving lives, and includes general oxygenation, high-race oxygen, non-invasive ventilator and sloping mechanical ventilation, depending on the severity of the disease.
Severe-respiratory patients with extracorporeal-pheroccupapulary membrane (ECMO), a cardiopulmonary alternative modified technique used for the treatment of severe cardiac or respiratory infirmity.
In addition, maintaining electrolyte balance, the prevention and treatment of secondary and septic shock infections, and the protection of vital organ functions are also essential for SARS-CoV-2 patients.
Cytocin cascade is known to result from the exacerbated reaction of the Inevolving immune system in SARS and MERS patients.
Cytocin cascade is a form of systemic inflammatory response, characterized by the release of a series of cytokines, including TNF, IL-1, IL-2, IL-6, IFN, IFN, IFN, and MCP-1.
These cytocules induce cells of the Inemoid system to release a large number of free-radicals, which are the main cause of ARDS and multiple organ failure.
Immunosuppression is essential in the treatment of cytokine cascades, especially in severe patients.
Corticosteroides and tocilizumab, a monoclonal Anti-IL6 Antibobody, are being used to treat cytokine cascades.
Other immunosuppressant treatments for the cytokine cascade include modulation of immune response directed to T-cells; blockade of IFN-y, IL-1 and TNF; inhibition of JAK, blinatumomab; cytokine signaling suppressor 4; and HDAC inhibitors.
Steroids such as immunosuppressants have been extensively used to treat SARS to reduce the severity of inflammatory damage.
However, high-dose steroids were not beneficial for severe lung injury in SARS and COVID-19 patients.
They may cause serious side effects, mainly avascular osteonecrosis, dramatically affecting the prognosis.
However, short periods of treatment with low-to-moderate low-dose corticosteroids have been recommended for co-cautious use in critically patient COVID-19 patients.
By the time this article is written, no effective antiviral therapy has been confirmed.
Intravenous administration with remdesivir, a nucleot-ideotid analogue, was found to have been found to be effective in an american COVID-19 patient.
remdesivir is a new antiviral drug developed by Gilead, initially aimed at the treatment of diseases caused by Ebola and Marburg viruses.
Remdesivir has also demonstrated a possible inhibition of other single-strand RNA RNA viruses, including MERS and SARS viruses.
Based on this, Gilead has provided the compost for China to carry out some trials in individuals infected with SARS-CoV-CoV-2, and the results are being long awaited.
In addition, baricitinib, interferon, lopinavir/ritonavir and ribavirina have been suggested as potential therapies for patients with acute respiratory symptoms.
Diarrhea, nause, vomit, liver damage and other adverse reactions may occur after combination therapy with lopinavir/ritonavir.
Interinterring these treatments with drugs used in patients should be closely monitored.
Plasma of Cured Patients and Antibode Generation
Mewage collection of patients who have cursued a contagious disease to treat other patients who suffer from the same disease, or to protect healthy individuals from contraction of the disease has a long history.
In fact, cured patients usually have a relatively high level of antibodies in the blood against the pathogen.
Antibodies are an immunoglobulin (lg) produced by B-lymph cells to combat pathogens and other foreign bodies, and they recognize unique molecules in pathogens and directly neutralize them.
Based on this, plasma was collected from the blood of a group of patients cursing COVID-, and was injected into 10 critically ill patients.
Their symptoms improved within 24 hours with the reduction of inflammation and viral loads and the improvement of oxygen s s saturation in the blood.
However, the verification and elucidation are necessary in order to propose the large-scale method before the development of specific therapies.
In addition, given therapeurapic effects, some convantages associated with plasma should be cautiously considered.
Antibodies can overstimulate the immune response and cause cytokine release syndrome, which is a life-threatening toxicity.
Antibodies concentration in the blood are usually low, and the demand for plasma is great to treat patients in a serious condition.
It's difficult to develop and produce specific antibodies quickly enough to combat an epidemic of global proportion.
It is therefore more important and more practical to insulate B-cells from cured patients and to identify genetic codes encoding effective antibodies or triage for effective anti-antibodies against essential virus proteins.
In this way we can rapidly increase the production of antibodies.
Traditional Chinese Medicine, TCM, has been used for thousands of years to treat a variety of ailments in China.
However, its effects largely depend on a multi-componponordinary combination in a formula that varies depending on a disease diagnosis based on TCM theories.
The majority of the effective components remain unknown, or are vague, since it is difficult to extract and verify these components or their optimal combinations.
Currently, due to the lack of specific and effective treatments for COVID-19, TCM has become one of the main alternative treatments for patients with mild or moderate symptoms, or for patients who have recovered from severe stages of the disease.
For example it has been discovered that Shu Feng Jie Du and Lian Hua Q Wen capsules are effective in treating COVID-19.
The best curhesion rates for COVID-19 patients were observed in a number of provinces in China who used TCM in 87% of patients, including Gansu (63.7%), Ningxia (50%), and Hunan (50%), while Hubei Prefecture, which used TCM in only about 30% of COVID-19 patients, obtained the lowest cure rate (13%).
However, this is a difficult comparison, since many impactive factors, such as the number of patients and severity of the disease, should be included in the evaluation.
On February 18, 2020, Boli Zhang and co-workers have published a comparative study of exclusive treatment with Western medicine and the combined treatment of MTC.
It turned out that the times necessary for the recovery of body temperature for the disappearance of symptoms and hospitalization were considerably lower in the group treated by Western medicine and TCM than only in the group treated by western medicine.
The rate of worsening of symptoms (from soft-to-refraid) was considerably lower for the group treated by Western and TCM treated for the group treated only by Western medicine (7.4% versus 46.2%), and mortality was lower in the group treated by Western medicine and ERM than in the group treated only by Western medicine (8.8% versus 39%).
However, efficiencies and TCM safety still awaits more duly controlled tests at larger scales and at more centers.
It would also be useful to characterize the mechanisms of action and to explain the effective components of TCM treatments or its combinations if possible.
Pre-confirmed patients with COVID-19 or with suspected disease generally feel great fear of highly contagious disease that may be fatal, and quarantine people also feel bored, lonely and irritated.
In addition, the symptoms of the infection such as fever, hypoalyxia and cough, as well as adverse treatment effects such as insomnia caused by corticosteroids can increase anxiety and psychological distress.
In the initial stage of the SARS epidemic, several psychiatric morbidities including persistent depression, anxiety, panic attacks, psycho-motor arousal, symptoms of psychosis, delirium and even suicidal tendencies have been reported.
Mandatory contact tracing and quarantine, as part of the actions of the public health system against COVID--19 epidemic, can make people more anxious, and they may feel guilty about the effects of contagion, quarantine, and stigma imposed on their families.
Therefore, mental health care should be provided to COVID-19 patients, people with suspected disease and people who have had contact with them, as well as the general population in need.
Psychological support should include the establishment of multidisciplinary mental health teams, the clear communication with regular and precise updates about the SARS-CoV-2 epidemic and treatment plans, and the use of professional electronic devices and applications to avoid direct contact between people.
Effective vaccinations are essential for disrupting the transmission chain of animal reservoirs and infected people to susceptible hosts, and are generally complementary to antiviral treatment in controlling epidemics caused by emergent-referider viruses.
“Initiatings have been taken to develop S-protect-based vaccines to generate sleighted and long-lasting neutralizing antibobodies and/or protection immunity against SARS-CoV.
Vaccinations have been evaluated in animal models for SARS.
However, the in vivo effectiveness of these vaccine candidates in older individuals and models of lethal challenge and their protection against zoonotic virus infection have yet to be determined before the start of a clinical study.
That’s probably due to the fact that SARS disappeared 17 years ago and no new cases have been reported since.
On the other hand, spilladic cases and foci of MERS continue to take place in the Middle East and spread to other regions, due to the persistence of zoonotic pelliters in endemic areas.
Vaccination Strategies have been developed for MERS using Inactive Viruse, DNA Plassidies, Viral Conveyors, NanoParticles, Virus-Similar Particles and Recombinant Protein Subunities, and some have been evaluated in animal models.
The development of a safe and effective SARS-CoV-2 vaccine for non-immune individuals is an urgent and essential task to control the current epidemic.
However, it's a challenge to overcome the difficulty, due to the long period (18 months, on an on average) necessary for the development of a vaccine and the variations in CoV dynamics.
Like a new illness, COVID-19 has just begun manifesting its complete clinical evolution, affecting thousands of patients.
In most of the cases, the patients gradually recover without sequelae.
However, just as SARS and MERS, COVID-19 is also associated with morbidity and high mortality in patients with severe cases.
Therefore, constructing a prognosis model for the disease is essential for health agencies to prioritise their services, particularly in areas with a shortage of resources.
Based on clinical studies reported to date, the following factors may affect or be associated with prognosis of COVID--19 (Treasure (Table33).
Age: the age was the most important factor for the prognosis of SARS, which also applies to COVID-19.
COVID-19 mainly affects people between 30 and 65 years, with 47.7% of the patients being over the age of 50, in an 8,8,866 case studies as described above.
Patients who required intensive treatment had a greater chance of presenting underlying comorbidities and complications, and were significantly older than patients who didn’t require intensive treatment (meacreased from 66 years versus 51), suggesting that age is a pro-prognosis factor for the health outcome of COVID-19 patients.
SARS-CoV-2 has infected more men than women (0.31/100,000 men and 0,27/100,000 women), as described above.
Embbivities and Complications: COVID-19 patients who require intensive treatment have a greater chance of acute heart injury or arthrhythmia.
Elecary episodes have been the leading cause of death in SARS patients.
SARS-CoV-CoV-2) has also been reported to be combined with ACL cholangi loangiocyte, which may lead to hepapatic disorders in COVID-19 patients.
It's important to note that age and underlying diseases have a strong correlation and may interfere with each other.
Absorbly laboratory results: The C-reactive protein (CRP) level in the blood reflects the seriousness of inflammation or injury to the Tissue and was proposed as a potential prognostic factor for the disease, response to therapy and final recovery.
Correlation of CRP level with severity and COVID-19 prognosis has also been proposed.
In addition, lactate hydrogenaase (LDH), aspartate aminotransferase (AST), alanine aminotransferase (ALT) and high creatine kinase (CK) may also assist in predicting the result.
These ensemble enzymes are present expressively in several organs, mainly in the heart and liver, and are released during tissue damage.
Therefore, they’re traditional markers for cardiac or hepatic disorders.
Primary clinical symptoms: chest X-ray and temporal progression of clinical symptoms should be considered in conjunction with other questions to predict COVID-19 outcomes/complications.
Stereroid use as described above, Steroids are immunosuppressants typically used as an adjunct therapy for infective diseases to reduce the severity of inflammatory damage.
Considering that a high corticosteroid dosage was widely used in patients with severe SARS, many survivors suffered abovascular osteonecrosworrm with permanent incapacitation and poor quality of life.
Therefore, if necessary, steroids should be used at a low dosage and for a short time in patients with COVID-19.
Psychological Stress: As described above, during the COVID-19 epidemic, many patients suffer from heightened stress since they go through long periods of quarantine and uncertainty and witness the death of detaineous members of the family and other patients.
It’s important to provide psychological counseling and end-to-lasting support to help these patients retrieve the stress and resume a normal life.
According to demographic studies so far COVID-19 seems to have epidemiological characteristics other than SARS.
In addition to replicating itself in the lower respiratory tract, SARS-CoV-2 manages to replicate efficiently in the upper respiratory tract and does not cause symptoms in the initial phase of infection (or causes only mild symptoms), in a similar way to other CoVs that cause common colds.
Therefore, patients who are infected in the initial phase or incubation period can produce a large amount of the virus during their daily activities, causing a great difficulty to control the epidemic.
In the meantime, it was considered that SARS-CoV transmission occurs when patients are severely ill, with the majority of transmutations not taking place in the initial phase.
Therefore, COVID-19's current epidemic is far more serious and difficult to control than the SARS epidemic.
Large efforts are currently underway in China, including the confinement of Wuhan and neighboring cities, and the continuous quarantine of almost the entire population, in the hope of halting transmission of SARS-CoV-2.
“Although these actions have drastically impacted the country’s economy and other sectors, the number of new patients is dwindling, indicating remedial of the epidemic.
The most optimistic estimate is that the epidemic will end by March, and the deceleration phase lasts between 3 and 4 months.
However, other experts aren't so optimistic.
“Paul Hunter, et al. has estimated that COVID-19, which appears to be substantially more infectious than SARS, won’t end in 2020.
Ira Longini, et al., established a model to predict the outcome of the epidemic and suggested that SARS-CoV-2 can infect two-thirds of the world's population.
A Canadian group reported that SARS-CoV-2 had been spotted in smeaslar smeasps in the throat and average turbinate of patients who had recovered and been discharged from the hospital in the previous 2 weeks, indicating that the new identified virus may become a cyccal episode similar to infg.
Promising indications took place in China based on the declining number of new cases, indicating that current strategies could be working.
It has originally been predicted to cause up to one million cases, with half a million deaths.
However, with strict quarantine and isolation, the disease was kept under control.
SARS-CoV-2 is possible in the same way that SARS-CoV may become weaker about its infectivity and, at some point, become a less pathogenic co-existing virus with humans.
Comparing the COVID--19 epidemic with SARS and MERS is below (Fig.
SARS-CoV-2 is highly transmissible by cough and sneeze, and possibly by direct contact with virus-contaminated materials.
The virus has also been found in faeces, which generates a new possibility: oral-fecal transmission.
A recent study of 138 cases reported that 41% of cases were possibly caused by nosocomial infections, including 17 patients with underlying diseases and 40 health professionals.
Therefore, greater precaution must be taken to protect people, especially health professionals, social workers, family members, co-workers and people with direct contact with infected patients or individuals.
The first line of defense that can be used to decrease the risk of infection is the use of face masks; the use of surgical masks and N95 respirator masquerade (series 1860s) help to control the propagation of the virus.
Surgical facial masks preclude fluid droplets of a potentially infected individual from being transported in thin air or attached to the surfaces of the objects where they can infect others.
In the meantime, only N95 masquerades (series No. 1860s) protect against inhale inhalation of villions with size of 10 nm to 80 nm, with only 5 percent of vírions being capable of penetrating it completely; SARS-CoV-2 is similar to SARS-CoV in in size, and the two have approximately 85 nm.
Since the particles can penetrate for up to five surgical masquerades together, health professionals in direct contact with patients must wear N95 masks (series No. 1860s), but not Surgical Masses.
In addition to masquerades, health professionals must use isolation robes to further reduce contact with the virus.
Viruse also infects an individual through the eyes.
On January 22, 2020, a doctor was infected with SARS-CoV-2, although he was wearing an N95 mask; the virus could have entered his body through his inflamed eyes.
Therefore, health care professionals should also wear transparent facial protectors or gosspers when serving patients.
For the general public in areas that are affected or potentially afflicted, it is highly recommended that everyone wash their hands with disinfective soap more often than usual, try to stay at home in isolation and limit contact with potentially infected people.
One metre is considered an adequate distance to stay away from a patient.
These actions are effective methods to reduce the risk of infection, as well as to prevent the spread of the virus.
Although SARS-CoV-CoV-2 has emerged as a new virus for humans, its SARS-CoV high homology, as reported on January 7, 2020 should have caused a major warning to China, based on its recent track record with the SARS epidemic in 2003.
However, it wasn’t until January 19, 2020, the director of Wuhan’s Center for Disease Control reassured citizens that the new virus has a low rate of contagion and limited reproducibility among humans, and that it was not difficult to prevent and contain the disease.
That message made people more relaxed, particularly when the entire country was preparing for the Spring Festival, and the critical period for the containment of the disease in Wuhan on a minimal scale was lost.
Encories control agencies in China can take advantage of this hard lesson and make crucial improvements in the future.
For example, these agencies must: (1) be more cautious in making public pronouncements, since everything they say is absorbed by the citizens and may influence their attitudes and decisions; (2) being more sensitive and re-aveactive to unusual information coming from hospitals, instead of expecting formal communications from physicians and authorities; (3) being more restrictive to contain a potential epidemic in its early stage, instead of trying to comfort the public.
COVID-19 Epidemic Caused by New SARS-CoV-2 Virus Started at the End of December 2019.
In less than two months, it has spread throughout China and nearly 50 other countries around the world, at the time of writing.
Since the virus is very similar to SARS-CoV and the symptoms are also similar between COVID-19 and SARS, COVID-19 epidemic has led to a sense of SARS return.
However, there are significant differences between COVID-19 and SARS, which are essential for the containment of the epidemic and the treatment of patients.
COVID-19 affects older people more than young people, and more men than women, and the severity and mortality rate are also higher in the elderly than in the young.
SARS has higher mortality than COVID-19 (10.91% versus 1.44%).
COVID-19 patients transmit the virus when they do not have symptoms, while SARS patients usually transmit it when they are severely ill, which generates a greater difficulty in curbing COVID-19 propagation than SARS.
This explains in part why SARS-CoV-2 propagates much faster and affects more regions than SARS-CoV.
Regular RNA assay for SARS-CoV-2 may be negative in some COVID-19 patients.
On the other hand, cured patients can receive a positive test for the virus again.
These results drastically increase the risk of spreading the virus.
Given the rapid progress in COVID-19 research, a number of crucial issues have yet to be resolved as follows:
From Where Did SARS-CoV-2 come From?
Although a 96% genetic counterpart has been found between SARS-CoV-2 and two SARS-like bat CoVs, we cannot yet conclude that SARS-CoV-2 originates from bats.
Which animal was the intermediate species that transmitted virus from the original host, the bat, let's say, to humans?
Unagadictable the answers to Questions No. 1 and 2, we will not be able to sever transmission efficiently, and the epidemic may re-decide at any time.
While molecular modeling and biochemical assays have demonstrated that SARS-CoV-2 joins ACE2, just how exactly does the virus go into respiratory cells and cause subsequent pathologic changes?
Is The Virus Also Aleccusing ACE-Expressing Cells In Other Organs?
With no clear answers to these questions, we cannot get a quick and accurate diagnosis and effective treatment.
How Long Will the Epidemic Last?
How is the virus genetically evolving during transmission between humans?
Is it going to become a worldwide pandemic, disappear like SARS or will it have seasonal recidivism, such as the flu?
It’s essential, but it may take some time to look for answers to the questions above and for so many others.
However, regardless of the necessary efforts, we have no other choice but to detain the epidemic as soon as possible and go back to our normal lives.
Zonotic Origins of Human Coronavirus
Mutation and adaptation have boosted co-evolution of Coronavirus (CoVs) and their hosts for thousands of years.
Prior to 2003, it was known that two human CoV (HCoVs) caused a mild illness, such as a common cold.
Epidemics of Severeal Respiratory Respiratory Syndrome (SARS) and Middle Eastern Respiratory Syndrome (MERS) have shifted what was thought about CoV and revealed how deadly and devastating HCoV HCoV infection can.
SARS-CoV-CoV-2 emergence in Central China at the end of 2019 put the CoVS in prominence again and stunned everyone with their high transmissibility and reduced pathogenicity compared to his brother SARS-CoV.
Infection with HCoV is zoonotic, and to understand the zoonotic origins of HCoVs is of great use.
The majority of HCoVs have originated from the bats, in which they are non-mopathogenic.
Interway reservoir hosts of some HCoVs are also known.
The identification of the animal hosts has direct implications for the prevention of diseases in humans.
Investigating CoV Host Interactions in Animals can also provide important information about CoV patogenesis in humans.
In this study, we present an overview of existing information about the seven HCoVs, with a focus on the history of their discovery as well as their zoonotic origins and interspecies transmission.
An important analysis is what we make of comparison and contrast between different HCoVs from a perspective of virus evolution and genome recombination.
The current disease epidemic by COV 2019 (COVID-19) is discussed in this context.
In addition, the requirements for an effective transition between hosts and the implications of the evolution of the virus in the severity of the disease are also highlighted.
Coronavirus (CoVs) belong to family Coronaviridae, which comprises a group of encapsulated viruses, of positive-voloter polarity and single-strand RNA.
These viruses that present the largest genome, between 26 and 32 kilobases, among RNA viruses were termed "CoVs" due to their crown  morphology when observed in an electron microscopocular.
Concerning structure, CoVs have unsegtaped genomes that share a similar organization.
Approximately two-thirds of the genome contain two overlapping open-label structures (ORF1a and ORF1b), which are transduced into replicase polyproteins pp1a and pp1ab.
Polyproteins are re-processed and generate 16 non-structural non-structuable proteins, called nsp1.
The remaining portion of the genome contains ORFs for structural proteins, including stupp spike (S), Envelope (E), Membra (M), and NucleoProtein (N).
Some ancillary-to-the-selection proteins are also encoded by different CoV lineages.
Based on the difference in protein sequences, CoVs are classified into four genera (alfaCoV, BetaCoV, gamma-CoV and deltaCoV), between which betaCoV genus contains the majority of HCoVs and is subdivided into four lineages (A, B, C and D).
phylogenectic evidence has shown that bats and rodents serve as a genetic source for the majority of alphaCoVs and beta-CoVs, while the birds are the main gamma ReservireCoVs and deltaCoVs.
“For thousands of years, CoVs have constantly interbreeded species barriers, and some have become relevant human pathogens.
To date, seven human CoVs (HCoVs) are known.
Among them HCoV-229E and HCoV-NL63 are alphaCoVs.
Others five betaCoVs include HCoV-OC43, HCoV-HKU1, severe Acute Respiratory Respiratory Syndrome Coronavirus (SARS-CoV), Middle Eastern Respiratory Syndrome Coronavirus (MERS-CoV), and SARS-CoV-2.
HCoV-229E, HCoV-OC43, HCoV-HKU1, and HCoV-NL63 viruses usually cause mild symptoms, such as a common cold and/or diarrhoea.
In contrast, SARS-CoV, MERS-CoV, and newly identified SARS-CoV-2 are highly pathogenic and cause severe lower respiratory tract infection in a relatively higher number of patients, with a high chance of developing acute respiratory distress syndrome (ARSD) and extrapulmonary manifestation.
B814 was isolated from corrise of HCoV--229, was isolated from corrone of patients with common chops in the mid-1960s.
Since then, more knowledge has accumulated through comprehensive studies on HCoV-229E and HCoV-OC, both of which cause self-re-limiting symptoms.
In fact, the concept that HCoV-infection is usually harmless was widely accepted until the SARS epidemic.
The SARS epidemic that took place in 2003 is one of the most devastating today and has infected more than  8,000 people, with a gross mortality rate related to confirmed cases of approximately 10%.
Ten years later, Middle Eastern Respiratory Syndrome (MERS) has resulted in a lingering epidemic in the Arabian Peninsula with sporedic propagating to the rest of the world.
The new HCoV of 2019 (2019–nCoV), which was later renamed SARS-CoV-CoV-2, is the causative agent of the 2019 coronavirus (COVID-19), which took over 3,120 lives and infected more than 91,000 by March 3, 2020.
The alarm is ringing, and the world needs to get ready for the SARS-CoV-2 pandemic that’s coming.
All seven HCoVs have zoonotic origin in bats, mice, or domestic animals.
Multiple lines of evidence corroborate the evolutionary origins of all HCoVs from the bats, where the viruses are well-adapted and non-fapathogenic but demonstrate great genetic diversity.
COVID-19 Epideciprocatics presents enormous medical, scientific, social, and moral challenges for China and the rest of the world.
Tracing the zoonotic origins of HCoVs provides a framework for understanding natural history, the driving force, and the restrictive factors of migrating the virus to other species.
This can also guide and facilitate the search for SARS-CoV-2's reservoir, interweattractor and amplifier, with important implications for the future prevention of cross-species viral migrae.
In this article we present an overview of zoonotic origins, inter-species and pathobeagenesis of HCoV.
In particular, we have highlighted and discussed the common theme that HCoV parental viruses are generally non-pathogenic in their natural reservoir hosts but become pathogenic after interspecies transmission to a new host.
We have also revised the evolution of HCoV, at which increased transmissibility is usually accompanied by decreased pathogenicity.
"The outcome of the current SARS-CoV-CoV-2 epidemic is also discussed in this context.
Animal CoVs have been known since the late 1930s.
Predose the first isolation of HCoV-229E strain B814 from the nozzle of patients who contracted a common cold, different CoVs had been insulated in several infected animals, including turkey, mice, cow, pork, cat and dog.
Over the last few decades, seven HCoVs have been identified.
A brief summary of HCoV discovery history in chronoological (Tabel 1) would be informative and instructive.
HCoV-229E was isolated from the respiratory tract of patients with upper respiratory tract infection in the year of 1966 and was later adapted for growth in Lung-38 cell lines.
HCoV-229E-infected patients had common cold symptoms, including headache, sneezing, malaise and sore-threw, with a fever and cough present in 10% to 20 percent of cases.
Subsequently, in 1967, HCoV-OC43 was isolated from organ culture and subsequent serial passage in mice's lactating brains.
The clinical features of HCoV-OC43 infection seem similar to those caused by HCoV-229E, which are symptomattically indistinguishable from infection by other respiratory tract pathogens, such as Influenza A Virus and rhinovirus.
HcoV-229E and HCoV-OC43 are distributed globally, and tend to be transmitted predominantly during the winter season in temperate climates.
In general the incubation period of these two viruses is shorter than one week, accompanied by the disease for approximately 2 weeks.
According to a study of human volunteers, healthy individuals infected with HCoV-229E have developed a common mild cold.
Only some immunocompromised patients exhibited severe lower respiratory tract infection.
SARS, also known as "atypical pneumonia," was the first documented pandemic caused by HCoV in the history of humans, and the etiological agent is SARS-CoV, the third HCoV discovered.
SARS' first case dates back to 2002 in Guangdong Province, China.
SARS epidemic resulted in 8,096 reported cases with 774 deaths, spreading to several countries and continents.
With the exception of "overscatterers," it has been estimated that each infected person can contaminate approximately two, with an incubation period of 4 to 7 days, and with the onset of peak the viral load on the tenth day of the disease.
Patients infected with SARS-CoV initially present myalgia, headache, fever, malaise and calfreres, followed by posterior symptoms of dyspnea, cough and respiratory discomfort.
Lymphopenia, hepatic dysfunction and high creatine kinase are common abnormalities of SARS in laboratory tests.
Diffusous alveolar damage, epitethelial cell proliferation and an increased number of macrophages are also observed in SARS patients.
Approximately 20 to 30 percent of the patients require, at some point, intensive treatment and mechanical ventilation.
In addition to the inferior respiratory tract, several organs, including  gastrointestinal tract, the liver and the kidneys, can also be affected in these severe cases, usually with the occurrence of a cytokine cascade, which can be lethal, mainly in immunocompromised patients.
The virus was first-included in a lung biopsy from a relative of the zero patient travelling from Guangzhou to Hong Kong.
Since then, huge efforts have been dedicated to HCoV research.
HCoV-NL63 was cordoned off from a 7-month-old in the Netherlands at the end of 2004.
It was initially discovered that the virus was prevalent in young children, the elderly and immunocompromised patients with respiratory diseases.
Nose, conjunctivitis, fefencing and bronquiolitis symptoms are common in disease caused by HCoV-NL63.
Another stand-alone study described the isolation of the same virus from a nasal specimen of an 8-month-old baby suffering from pneumonia in the Netherlands.
Although it has been identified in the Netherlands it is dispersed around the world.
HCoV-NL63 was estimated to correspond to approximately 4.7% of common respiratory diseases, and its peak incidence occurs in the winter, in the spring and at the beginning of summer.
HCoV-NL63 is associated with obstructive laryngitis, which is also known as crupe.
In the same year HCoV-HKU1 was cordoned off from a 71-year-old hospitalized with pneumonia and bronquiolite in Hong Kong.
In addition to conjuni pneumonia and broncho-encloseluitis acquired in a community, it was reported that HCoV-HKU was associated with exacerbation of acute asthma.
Like HCoV-NL63, HCoV-229E and HCoV-OC43, HCoV-HKU1 has been found worldwide, causing mild respiratory diseases.
All of these community-acquired HCoVs adapted well to humans and are generally less likely to mutate and cause highly pathogenic diseases, although accidents occurred for unknown reasons in the rare case of a more virulent sub-type of HCoV-NL63, which has recently been reported as causing serious lower respiratory tract infection in China.
In general, when these HCoVs acquire the ability to be transmitted in an efficient way and to continuously remain in humans, they also become less virulent and less pathogenic.
MERS-CoV was first-elected isolated in 2012 from a 60-Year-Old Patient Who Proposed Acute Acute Pneumoles and Kidney Failure in Saudi Arabia.
While most laboratory-confirmed cases originate in the Middle East, imported cases with occasional secondary spread to nearby contacts have been reported in several European countries and in Tunisia.
Another secondary epidemic took place in South Korea in 2015 with 186 confirmed cases.
MERS clinical manifestations are similar to SARS, characterized by progressive acute pneumonia.
Unlike SARS, many patients with MERS also develop acute kidney failure, which is therefore very peculiar to MERS, among diseases caused by HCoV.
More than 30% of the patients have   Gastrointestinal symptoms such as diarrhoea and vomit.
As of Feb. 14, 2020, over 2,500 laboratory-confirmed cases have been reported with a high mortality rate related to confirmed cases of 34.4%, which made MERS-CoV one of the deadliest viruses.
From half to the end of December 2019, groups of patients with pneumonia, who were retrospectively known to be associated with SARS-CoV-2 infection were detected in Wuhan, Hubei Province in China.
"The World Health Organization has declared the continuing epidemic of lower respiratory tract infection caused by SARS-CoV-CoV-2 a Public Health Emergence of International Importance and has also appointed COVID-19.
Until March 3, 2020, 9,5,053 cases have been confirmed worldwide, with a gross mortality rate related to confirmed cases of 3.4%.
It should be noted that the lethality related to confirmed cases in Hubei in China is 4.2%, while the lethality outside this city is 1.2%.
SARS-CoV-CoV-2 causes serious respiratory infection, in the same way as SARS-CoV and MERS-CoV, with the presence of fever, cough and dyspnoea.
Some of the patients also have diarrhoea.
Pneumoles are one of the most severe symptoms and can rapidly develop into acute respiratory distress syndrome.
While SARS-CoV and SARS-CoV-CoV-2 are very similar due to the high homology of 82% in nucleotide sequence, they are grouped into different branches in phylogenetic trees.
SARS-CoV-CoV-2 appears to be less pathogenic but is more transmissible compared with SARS-CoV and MERS-CoV.
Assymptomatic SARS-CoV-2-infected individuals have been reported, and they may contribute to the rapid spread of the virus around the world.
Comparations and contrast of SARS-CoV-CoV-2 with the other six HCoVs reveal similarities and differences of great relevance.
First of all, the incubation period, and the duration of the evolution of HCoV disease are very similar.
SARS-CoV-2 follows the overall trend of six other HCoVs.
In the second place the severity of COVID-19 symptoms arises between the seriousness of SARS-CoV and the seriousness of the four Community-acquired HCoV (HCoV-229E, HCoV-OC43, HCoV-HKKU1 and HCoV-NL63).
On the one hand, SARS-CoV-2 infection presents characteristics that are more observed during infection with HCoVs acquired in a community way, including the presence of non-shospecific, mild or even the absence of symptoms.
On the other hand, a small sub-sample of severe COVID-19 cases can also be observed, such as SARS-CoV-infection, although the proportion is slightly lower.
In third place, SARS–CoV-2 transmission also demonstrates interesting standards, both in the HCoVs acquired in a community way and in SARS-CoV.
On the for one, the transmissibility of SARS-CoV-2 is at at least as high as that of HCoV-acquired in the community.
On the other hand, it should be confirmed whether SARS-CoV-2 transmissibility decreases after switching to humans, such as SARS-CoV and MERS-CoV.
Thus, just like other HCoV, SARS-CoV-2 may be detected in fecal samples.
It has yet to be clarified in future studies if oro-fecal transmission of SARS-CoV-2 performs an important role, as in SARS-CoV at least in some circumstances.
It's also very interesting to find out if SARS-CoV-CoV-2 may exhibit seasonality, such as in cases of community-required HCoV.
Either way, the characteristics of SARS-CoV-2, including their transmissibility, pathogenicity and sustainable propagation after their passage to humans, will influence the outcome of the current COVID-19 epidemic.
All HCoV-acquired by communist mode that cause mild symptoms adapted well to humans.
From another point of perspective, it may also be true that human beings have adapted well to these four HCoVs.
In other words, the two of them could be the survivors of HCoV ancient pandemics.
HCoVs that cause serious diseases in humans and to people who develop serious HCoV diseases have been eliminated.
For this to happen, HCoVs need to be replicated in humans to a sufficient extent in order to allow the accruing of adaptive mutations that neutralize restriction factors.
In this sense, the longer the permanence time of SARS-CoV-2 epidemic, the more people will be infected, and the greater the chance that the virus will adapt to humans.
If the adaptation is adequate, its transmission to humans it would be difficult to control quarantine or other infection-control measures.
For a number of years, the four CoV-acquired communally circulates in human populations, causing common cold in immunocompetent individuals.
These viruses don’t require an animal reservoir.
On the other hand, SARS-CoV and MERS-CoV, highly pathogenic, did not adapt well to being human, and its transmission among humans cannot be sustained.
They need to maintain and propagate in its zoonotic reservoirs and seize the chance of migrating to susceptible human targets, possibly through one or more inter-intermediate and amplifying hosts.
SARS-CoV-CoV-2 has characteristics similar to SARS-CoV, MERS-CoV and the four HCoVs acquired in a community.
It is highly communicable, in the same way as HCoVs acquired in a community way, at least so far.
However, it’s more pathogenic than communally-acquired HCoV and fewer pathogenic than SARS-CoV and MERS-CoV.
It still needs to be discovered if it will fully adapt to humans and circulate between us without a reservoir animal host.
Before we discuss the animal origins of HCoVs, it would be useful to discuss the definitions and characteristics and characteristics of evoluctive hosts, natural reservoirs, intermarminors and amplifier.
An animal serves as the evolutionary host of a HCoV if it harbors an ancestor of direct relationship, sharing high homology at the nucleotid sequence level.
Ancespheral virus is usually well-adapted and non-pathogenic in this host.
In the same way, a reservoir host houses HCoV in a continuous and end-lasting way.
In both cases, the hosts are naturally infected and are the natural hosts of HCoV or their parental virus.
By contrast, if HCoV is newly introduced to an intermediary host just before or close to its introduction to humans, it does not adapt properly to the new host and is usually pathogenic.
This inter-intermediate host may serve as the zoonotic source of human infection and perform the function of an amplifier host by allowing the virus to transiiently and then transmitting it to humans and increasing the scale of human infection.
An HCoV may have an infection disrupted if it fails to sustain its transmission in the interweaving host.
By contrast, HCoVs can also adapt to the inter-intermediate host and establish long-term endemicicity.
In this case, the interweaving host becomes the natural reservoir host.
