Virus-host interaction in relation to transmission
In addition to the three viral factors mentioned above, viral interaction with the host receiver is another key factor that influences interspecble transmission.
The re-combination of SARS-CoV is taken as a typical example, which has also shown evidence of positive selection during interspecies transmission events.
Drawing on the comparative analysis of SARS-CoV isolates of humans and civetes, SARS-CoV is believed to undergo rapid adaptation in different hosts, especially with mutations in the DLR of the S-protein.
In general, DLR in a CoV-in-Greet protein interacts with the cell receptor and is intensely selected by host antibobody response.
At SARS-CoV, DLR is in amino acids 318 to 510o in fragment S1, which attaches to human ACA2, as well as its co-correceptors for viral input.
SARS-CoV DLR is able to recognize ECA2 receptors of several animals, including bats, civets, mice and raccoin dogs, enabling interspecies transmission of the virus.
In fact, it was observed that only six amino acid residues were different from human viral isolates and civetes in the DLR, and four of them are located in the receptor binding motif for interaction with the ECA2 receptor.
SARS-CoV from civets has K4.79N and S487T mutates in their DLR, which could increase the affinity of the spike protein interaction with the human ACL receptor.
In other words, these two subjections of amino acids could be critical for viral adaptation in humans.
SARS-CoV-2 shares the same cellular receiver with SARS-CoV.
A difference of 30% between SARS-CoV-CoV and SARS-CoV on the S-protein unit implies that the binding affinity of its S-letter protein and human ACS could have been altered.
In fact, a study with electron cryomicroscopy indicated an affinity of 10 to 20 times greater of this bond than between human ACS and SARS-CoV S-protein.
It will also be useful to determine whether any other co-receiver may be necessary for transmission of SARS-CoV-CoV-2.
