The Study of CoVs in Bats and Other Animals Dramatically Changed Our Perceptions about the Importance of Zoonotic Origins and Animal Reservoirs From HCoVs in Transmission Between Humans.
Extensive evidence show that SARS-CoV, MERS-CoV, and SARS-CoV-CoV-2 have their origin in the bat and are transmitted to people through inter-intermediate hosts.
Given that SARS-CoV infection originated from the contact between humans and civetes in the markets, the closure of wet markets and the culling of civets in these markets could have put an end to SARS epidemic.
Following the same rationale, pangolins should be removed from wet markets to prevent zonotic transmission, given the discovery of multiple lineages of pangolin pangolos closely related to SARS-CoV-2.
However, it should still be clarified in future studies if SARS-CoV-2 is transmitted to humans through pangolins and from other mammals, and the mode of such transmission.
On the other hand, MERS-CoV has existed in dromedaries for a long time.
Such camels serve as an important means of transport, and are an essential source of meat, milk, leather, and leol for the local community.
They're widely distributed in the Middle East and in Africa.
It is therefore impossible to sacrifice all camels for MERS control, as has been done at wild animal markets in China to thie affliction of SARS-CoV and SARS-CoV-2.
To defraite MERS-recurring epidemics, a comprehensive approach must be adopted to develop effective MERS-CoV vaccines for camels in combination with other infection control measures.
Since we are unable to eliminate these viruses, new genotypes may arise and cause epidemics.
A variety of zoonotic CoVs circulate in nature.
In fact, CoV of bats with zoonotic potential is very diverse.
There are a number of possibilities for zoonotic CoVs to evolve and re-combine, resulting in the emergence of new CoVs that are more transmissible and/or lethal to humans in the future.
The culture of eating wild animals in some parts of China must be abandoned to reduce unnecessary contact between humans and animals.
With SARS, MERS and COVID-19 crisis, a better response and preparedness plan should be adopted.
A lot of viruses exist on the planet for a long time.
They stay in their natural reservoirs until there’s a chance of migrating to another species.
Although the bats have many characteristics that favor the propagation of the virus, the chance that people will be in contact with bats and other wild species can be minimized if people are taught to stay away from them.
Concontinued vigilation in mammals is necessary for a better understanding of the ecology of CoVs and their natural hosts, which will prove to be useful in the prevention of the transmission of animals to humans.
In conclusion, the most effective way to prevent viral zoonosis is for people to stay away from the ecological niches of natural zoonotic virus reservoirs.
Several specimens of jigsaw the zoonotic origin of SARS-CoV-CoV-2 are still lacking.
First, if the bats transmitted an ancestral SARS-CoV-2 virus to pangolonins, it’s important to find out under what circumstances bats and pangolosins could share the same ecological niche.
In the second place, if bats play a more direct role in transmitting to human beings should be determined how people come into contact with the bats.
Third, if a third memoma has the role of the true intermediate host, it needs to be clarified the way in which he interacts with different species, including humans, bats and pangolins.
Finally, since a number of mammals, including domestic animals, may be susceptible to SARS-CoV-2, both vigilance and experimental infection should be performed.
Whether it is a bat, pangolin, or other mammal, SARS-CoV-2 or its nearly identical parental viruses is expected to be identified in their natural hosts in the future.
Continuing research in this area will elucidate the evolutionary route of SARS-CoV-2 in animals, with important implications in the prevention and control of COVID-19 in humans.
It is necessary to update the "suspicious case" and "confessed case" of COVID-19
On February 6, 2020, our team published a quick guide of guidance for the diagnosis and treatment of infection of new coronavirus 2019 (2019-nCoV). This guide imparts our expertise and constitutes a good reference for combating this worldwide pandemic.
However, Coronavirus Disease of 2019 (COVID-19) is New. Our understanding and knowledge gradually increase based on the constant findings of research and experiences in clinical practice; therefore, diagnostic and treatment strategies are also continuously up-to-probed.
In this letter we respond to a commentary on our guide and provide the most recent “suspicious incase” and “confessed case” according to the latest COVID-19 Diagnosis and Treatment Guides (Seventh Version) published by the National Health Committee of the People’s Republic of China.
In December of 2019, the New Corovirus of 2019 (2019-nCoV) caused an outbreak, which is officially referred to now as Coronavirus Disease of 2019 (COVID-19), and the virus is called Corovirus 2 of Severe Acute Respiratory Syndrome (SARS-CoV-2).
On March 11, 2020, WHO classified COVID-19 a pandemic.
To combat SARS–CoV-2 infection, our team has developed a Quick Guidance Guidance Adgamation Published Online in Military Medical Research on February 6, 2020.
He's been attracting excellent attention since publishing.
However, note that COVID-19 is a new disease. Our understanding and knowledge gradually increase based on the constant findings of research and experiences in clinical practice; hence, diagnostic and treatment strategies are also continuously updated.
For example, COVID-19 Diagnosis and Treatment Guides published by the National Health Committee of The People's Republic of China (http://www.nhc.gov.cn/) between January 16, 2020 and March 3, 2020, have a total of seven edits with substantial changes in the context in some.
Our guide has now received a commentary from Zhou et al., who presented a simple scoring proposition based on his clinical experience.
This work has added new pieces of evidence to our guide and also makes valuable references to this worldwide pandemic.
We end-worm of her significant work and express our gratitude.
However, his work also needs to be updated according to the latest COVID-19 Diagnosis and Treatment Guides (seventh-repalace version) and recent studies.
Pursuant to the seventh edition (March 3, 2020), in order to confirm the suspected case, it is necessary to combine an epidemiological history item with two items of clinical manifestations to make a comprehensive analysis, or it is necessary to fulfill three items of clinical manifestation, if there is no clear epidemiological history:
Epidemiological history: (1) a history of travel or residence in Wuhan city and in nearby areas or in other communities where COVID-19 cases have been reported for the past 14 days prior to the onset of symptoms; (2) a history of contact with ininfectious SARS-CoV-CoV-2 cases (with positive Nucleic Acid test); (3) a history of contact with patients with fever or respiratory symptoms coming from Wuhan's city and nearby areas.
Clinical manifestations: (1) Fever and/or respiratory symptoms; (2) with imaging resources of COVID--19 infection; (3) total white blood cell count exhibiting normal, diminished or reduced lymphocyte count at the early stage of onset of symptoms.
Diagnosing the confirmed case should be based on a suspected case with any of the following serologic or pathogenic items: (1) PCR-positive examination for SARS-CoV-2; (2) sequencing of the complete viral genome exhibiting high homogeneity in relation to known new coronaviruses; (3) positive for IgM antibodes specific for SARS-CoV-CoV-2 in serum examination; or some alteration of the antibomb IgG-specific for SARS-Co V-2
You can note that real-time PC R&B for nucleic accid in blood or respiratory tract samples has been added to the second and third edition (January 18 and 22, 2020, respectively).
The pathogenic detection of the blood sample was added to the fourth and fifth edition (from January 27, 2020 and February 8, 2020, respectively), and sorological evidence was added to the seventh edition.
These modifications are based on the continuous work of researchers in the search for an ideal nucleic acid detection kit for rapid diagnosis and samples of the respiratory tract, including blood sampling, which has increased the availability of different specimens and helped to bring the positive result of specific antibodies to confirmed criteria.
In addition, more and more pieces of evidence that remind us to pay attention to symptomatic and asymptomatic atypical patients.
Because of this, the flowchart of Zhou et al. should be updated because it classifies people without clinical symptoms as "low-de-esight".
punctuation system also needs to be verified in more studies and clinical practices.
In conclusion we look forward to the emergence of further direct evidence and ask that readers provide their comments.
To diagnose “supt case” and “confirmed case,” we suggest that they verify and comply with the most recent guidelines of their countries of origin.
Our team will also timelyly update our guide to offer help.
Bangladesh Report Five New Deaths Due to COVID-19, Highest Daily Number
Yesterday, Bangladesh confirmed five new deaths due to COVID-19 on the day.
That’s the highest death toll in a day due to the virus.
Yesterday, the Institute of Epidemiology, Disease Control, and Drug Research (IEDCR) reported that the number of registered confirmed cases included 114 active cases and 33 retrieved retrieved remaining at home.
A total of 17 deaths have been recorded.
According to an online news, IEDCR Director Dr Meerjady Sabrina Flora said the killings included four men and one woman.
According to Meerjady, two cases were over the age of 60, two were aged between the ages of 51 and 60, and one was between the ages of 41 and 50.
She also said two of the victims were from Dhaka.
The World Health Organization (WHO) declared COVID-19 pandemic on March 11.
A hospital official told Anadolu Agency, a local news agency, that one of those killed was Jalhal Saifur Rahman, director of Bengali Anti-Corruption Commission who was treated at Kuwait Maitree Hospital.
On Saturday, in an on-video online statement, the Bengali road transport minister Obaidul Quader bridges said public transportation would be paralysed for more time than originally planned until next Saturday.
This paralysis of public transportation began on March 26 and was planned to end on Saturday, April 4.
The transportation of essential products (medical products, fuel and food) was still permitted.
The first COVID-19 infection incidents in Bangladesh were recorded on March 8, in two people who have returned from Italy and also to the wife of one of them.
By March 19, all of them had already recovered.
