Stores in Australia cut toilet paper limits per transaction
On Saturday night and on Sunday, Australian store chains Woolworths and Coles have reduced restrictions on the purchase of toilet paper, respectively, for two packages and one package per transaction in all stores in the country.
On Monday ALDI also put in place a pay-up limit.
These limitations have been communicated by messages in the boxes and on the Facebook pages of the networks.
For fear of COVID-19, shoppers were supposedly stocking up in case there was a need to insulate themselves.
On Wednesday, Woolworths also limited purchases of home-delivegoing toilet paper for one parcel-per-an-an-an-on-order.
These changes came after the foregoing restriction of four packets per transaction implemented by Woolworths and Coles on March 4 and March 5, respectively.
Coles, in its March 8 press release, reported that even with the restriction of four packages, “many stores are still deshausting inventories by less than an hour after delivery” and called demand “unprecedented,” while ALDI called it an “unexpected” in a Facebook post on Tuesday.
Same sales had a "sharp increase" last week, according to a Woolworths spokesman.
The Costco Store in Canberra has also limited the amount allowed for two packages last week.
To further elicit scarcity, Coles has commissioned larger packages from vendors and increased delivery frequency, Woolworths commissioned extra inventory, while ALDI has previously available inventories of a planned promotion for Wednesday.
Russel Zimmerman, chief executive officer of the Australian Retailers Association, said retailers were trying to increase inventories, but that restrictions of the local board in relation to the frequency of deliveries by truck hampering.
He expects high production costs, as suppliers try to meet the demand and fewer promotions.
On Tuesday, ALDI announced that, after the early release of the stock, some stores will not be able to carry out Wednesday's promotion.
According to News.com.au, Dr. Gary Mortimer, a retail specialist at Queensland University of the University of Technology, has said the stores replenish stock every night.
He pointed out that toilet paper is a voluminous item with numbers low in stock in terms of quantity, and that, when exhausted, leaves many empty spaces on the shelves, increasing the feeling of scarcity.
Coles and The Woolworths have an opinion that if there was an abundant amount on shelves, if products such as hygienic paper rolls and antiseptics they could be [accomplished] and are there in quantities, you’d probably reduce panic, Russell Zimmerman told ABC News.
Recycled toilet paper maker Who Gives to Crap said on Wednesday they would be out of stock.
Kimberly-Clark, which produces Kleenex Sanitary Role and Solaris Paper, which produces Sorbent, have emphasized that they were working 24 hours a day/7 days a week to maintain supply, according to a report by News.com.au.
Realbiblical website domain.com reported that some real estate sellers were offering free toilet paper to the first auction bidders in Melbourne, while fewer auctions were being held with shoppers taking a slack on a prolonged Labor Day weekend.
Thursday's issue of NT News, a diary print by Darwin, included an eight-page embezzles with the aim of being cropped and used as toilet papers.
Shops were initially reluctant to enforcing restrictions, according to an ABC Australia report on March 3 in which they said they had no plans to implement restrictions on purchases.
Russell Zimmerman added that other products are also in high demand such as masks, antiseptics, dry products, hand soap and flour.
Same as in Australia on Sunday evening, the British online supermarket Ocado has limited purchases of Andres toilet paper for two 12 reel packages.
The World Health Organization Declares COVID-19 Compandemic
The World Health Organization (WHO) on Wednesday reported that the current COVID--19 outbreak — the disease caused by SARS-CoV-2 coronavirus — is a pandemic.
While the word “pandemic” indicates only the degree of the spread of a disease, not how periloutable the specific cases are, the WHO emphasized the need to prominunate governments to act:
All countries can still change the course of this pandemic.
"If countries are able to detect, test, treat, isolate, track and mobilise the population for a response," said Tedros Adhanom Ghebreyesus, Director-in-Child, Director-in-Chief.
"We're very preoccupied with the alarming levels of dissemination and gravity and the alarming levels of lack of action.
According to Tom Frieden, a former director of the United States Centers for Disease Control and Prevention, is an “unprecedented” pandemic.
"Owing with influenza, no other respiratory viruses have been monitored from emerging to continuous global spread."
Ghebreyesus expressed a similar position, stating that we had never seen a pandemic initiated by a coronavirus.
“We’ve never seen a Pandehomic That Could Be Controlled At the Same Time.”
The New Pandehomic Status Follows WHO's January decision to declare the outbreak a public health Emergency of International Concrete.
U.S. National Allergy and Infectious Occur (U.S.) Director Dr. Anthony Fauci said the outbreak, "in short, will get worse."
On Thursday, the Associated Press said there were at least 126,000 COVID-19 cases worldwide with over 4,600 deaths.
The 2019–20 Coronavirus Pandehomic is a continuous pandemic of coronavirus disease 2019 (COVID-19), caused by coronavirus of Severe Acute Respiratory Syndrome 2 (SARS-CoV-2).
The outbreak was identified in Wuhan, China in December 2019, was declared an international public health emergency of international concern on January 30, 2020 and recognized as pandemic on March 11, 2020.
By April 10, 2020, approximately 1.61 million COVID-19 cases had been reported in 2,110 countries and territories, with approximately 97,000 deaths.
About 3,34,000 people have retrieved.
The mortality rate is estimated to be 4% in 4% in China, while globally it ranges from 11.04% in Algeria to 0.08% in New Zealand.
Common symptoms include fever, a cough, and blude of breath.
Complications may include pneumonia and acute respiratory distress syndrome.
The time from exposure to the onset of symptoms is about five days but can vary from two to fourteen days.
There’s no specific vaccine or anti-viral treatment.
The primary treatment is symptomatic and supportive therapy. Recommended preventive measures include washing the hands, covering the mouth when coughing, keeping distance from people, and monitoring and isolating people with suspected infection.
Authorities from all over the world responded by implementing travel restrictions, quarantines, curfews, workplace risk controls, and closure of establishments.
"The pandemic has provoked severe global socio-economic outroaching, postponing or cancelling sporting, religious, political, and cultural events and widespread shortage of supplies exacerbated by panic-triggered purchases.
Schools and universities closed at national or local level in 193 countries, afflicting approximately 99.4% of the world’s student population.
Misinfective about the virus has spread online and incidents of xenofoophobia and anti-discrimination have taken place against Chinese people, people who are promenented and similar to East and Southeast Asia and others in areas with significant cases of the virus.
Due to the reduction of travel and the closures of the heavy industry there has been a decrease in air pollution and carbon emissions.
Sanitary officials from Wuhan in China (the Capital of Hubei Province) have reported a group of pneumonia cases due to unknown cause on Dec. 31, 2019, and an investigation was launched in early January 2020.
To a large extent, the cases were linked to Huanan's wholesale seafood market and, therefore, the virus is believed to have zoonotic origin.
Surressage-causing virus is known as SARS-CoV-V-2, a recently discovered virus that is closely linked to bat coronavirus, pangolin and SARS-CoV. The first-in-a-know-of-suggested symptoms on December 1, 2019 and did not present visible connections with the subsequent group of the seafood market.
Of the initial group of cases reported in December of 2019, two-thirds were found to be linked to the market.
On March 13, 2020, an unveritable report from the South China Morning Post suggested that a case pointed to November 17, 2019, in a 55-Y-Y-Of-Y-Of-Hurged Individual from Hubei Province, which may have been the first. By February 26, 2020, the WHO reported that, as newly reported cases declined in China but suddenly increased in Italy, Iran and South-South Korea, the number of New Cases Outside China Had Surpassed The Number of New Cases in China
Significant under-relatification may occur, especially among people with milder symptoms.
By February 26, relatively few cases had been reported among 19-year-over-year-old, accounting for 2.4 per cent of cases worldwide. Britain's leading scientific consultant, Patrick Vallance, estimated that 60% of the British population would need to be become infected until effective collective immunity could be achieved.
The cases refer to the number of people who have been tested for COVID-19 and whose tests have been confirmed positive in accordance with the official protocols.
By March 23, no country had tested more than 3 per cent of its population and several countries adopted official policies not to test people with only mild symptoms, such as Italy, the Netherlands, Spain, and Switzerland.
A study published on March 16 found that by January 23, it was an estimated 86% of COVID-19 infections had not been detected and that these unrabequeted infections were the source of infection of 79% of documented cases.
A statistical analysis published on March 30 estimated that the number of people infected in Italy was considerably higher than reported cases.
Initial estimates of the basic reproduction number (R0) for COVID-19 were 1,4 to 2.4.
A study published by the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention concluded it may be 5.7.
Most people with COVID-19 recover.
According to those who do not recover, time from the onset of symptoms to death has been between 6 and 41 days, being 14 days the most common.
Until April 10, 2020, approximately 97,000 deaths were attributed to COVID-19.
In China, by February 5, approximately 80 percent of deaths occurred in people older than 60 and 75% had pre-existing medical conditions, including cardiovascular diseases and diabetes. The official death toll from COVID-19 pandemic usually refers to people who died and who tested positive for COVID, in accordance with the official protocols.
The actual death toll from COVID-19 can be much higher, since it may not include people who have passed away without having done the test, for example, who have died at home, in nursing homes, etc.
Partial data from Italy have demonstrated that the additional death toll during the Pandehomic Surfecturated the Official Count of COVID deaths by a factor of 4–5.
Spokeswoman for U.S. Disease Control & Prevention (CDC) acknowledged that "we know [the official death toll] is underappointed," a statement corroborated by episodic under-remportation reports in the U.S. This underestimation often occurs in pandemics, such as H1N1 swine's flu epidemic in 2009.
The first death outside China took place on February 1 in the Philippines, and the first death outside of Asia took place in France on February 14.
By Feb. 28 outside of China, more than a dozen deaths had already been recorded in Iran, South Korea, and Italy.
As of March 13, over for forty countries and territories had reported deaths on all continents except in Antarctica. Several measurements are commonly used to quantify mortality.
These numbers vary according to the region and over time and are influenced by the volume of tests, quality of the health care system, treatment options, time from the initial outburgrment and the characteristics of the population, such as age, sex and general health. The coefficient of fatality represents the number of deaths divided by the number of cases diagnosed in a given interval.
Drawing on John's Hopkins University stats, overall fatality coefficient is 6.0% (97,039/1,1,617,204) by April 10, 2020.
The number varies depending on the region.
In China estimating the fatality coefficient dropped from 17.3% (in people with onset of symptoms between 1–January December 1, 2020) to 0.7% (for people with onset of symptoms after February 1, 2020). Other measures include the mortality rate (CFR), which represents the percentage of people diagnosed who die due to an illness, and the mortality rate of infection (IFR), which accounts for the percentage of undiagnosed (diagnos).
These statics are not static in time and follow a specific population of the infection by resolving the case.
Some scholars have attempted to calculate these numbers for specific populations.
The Centre for Evidence-Based Medicine at the University of Oxford estimates that the mortality rate of infection for the pandemic in general is between 0.1% and 0.39%.
The upper estimate of this interval is compatible with the results of the first random trial for COVID-19 in Germany and with a statistical study that analyzed the impact of tests on CFR estimates.
The WHO says the pandemic can be brought under control.
The peak and maximum duration of the outbreak are uncertain and may change depending on location.
Maciej Boni of Pennsylvania State University stated that “Without due control, infectious epidemics usually reach its peak and then go into decline when there are no more hosts available for the disease.
“It’s almost impossible to do any proper projection at the moment about when it will occur.”
Zhong Nanshan, a Senior Medical Adviser of the Chinese government, said it "could end by June if all countries mobilize to follow WHO recommendations regarding measures to deprive the spread of the virus.
Adam Kucharski of London School of Hygiene and Tropical Medicine declared SARS-CoV-2 "probably to remain in circulation for a year or two".
According to an Imperial College study, led by Neil Ferguson, physical distancing and other measures will be necessary "until a vaccine is available (potentially within 18 months or more).
William Schaffner of Vanderbilt University said: "I find it improbable that this coronavirus will completely disappear as it is highly transcommunicable" and "which could become a seasonal disease by reappearing every year."
Transulence of reappearance will depend on group immunity and the extent of the mutation.
COVID-19 symptoms may be relatively non-compecific and infected people may be assymptometic.
The most common symptoms are fever (88 percent) and dry cough (68 percent).
Less common symptoms include fatigue, production of respiratory secretion (catarro), loss of olfactory sensation, shortness of breath, muscle and joint pain, sore throat, headache, calfreres, vomit, hemoptysis, diarrhoea or cyanosis. WHO states that approximately one in six people becomes seriously ill and has difficulty breathing.
The US Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CKD) lists that emergencies symptoms are difficulty inbreathing, persistent chest pain or pressure, sudden confounding, difficulty gazing and blue-colored face or lips. It is advised to seek medical attention if these symptoms are present. The evolution of the disease can cause severe pneumonia, acute respiratory distress syndrome, sepsis, septic shock and death.
Some infected people may be asymptomatic, with no clinical symptoms, but with test results that confirm the infection, so the researchers recommend that those who have been in close contact with a person with confirmed infection are monitored and examined closely to rule out the infection.
Chinese estimates of assymptomatic rafts vary from a few to 44%.
The usual in incubation period (the time between the onset of infection and symptoms) ranges from one to 14 days, but it is usually five days. As an example of the uncertainty, the estimation of people with COVID-19 who lost the olfactory sensation was initially 30% and subsequently dropped to 15%.
Some details about how the disease spread is still being determined.
It is believed that this disease is disseminated mainly due to close contact and by small droplets produced during the act of cough, sneezing or talking, and close contact is considered from 1 to 2 meters (3 to 6 feet).
Studies have found that coughing without covering your mouth can cause droplets to range from 5.5 meters (15 feet to 8.2 metres (27 ft).
Some people have proposed that the virus can also be transmitted by droplets that remain for longer periods in the air, which may have been generated during a conversation. Breast droplets can also be produced during exhalation, including when talking, although the virus is not normally suspended.
Guesculates may land on the mouths and noses of those close to them, or may be inhaled by the lungs.
Some medical procedures such as cardiopulmonary resuscitation (CRP) may cause nebulization of respiratory secretions, resulting in propagation by air.
Propagation can also occur when someone touches a contaminated surface, including the skin, and then touches its own eyes, nose or mouth.
“Whether there is a precaution that it can spread through feces, it is believed that the risk of it happening will be low.
The Chinese government has denied the possibility of oro-fecal transmission of SARS-CoV-2. The virus is more contagious during the first three days after symptoms appear, although transmission may be possible before symptoms appear and at later stages of the disease.
There have been positive tests for the disease for up to three days before the onset of symptoms, which suggests that transmission is possible even before the development of significant symptoms.
There are only a few asymptomatic case-confirmed reports, but assymptomatic transmission has been identified in some countries during contact tracing investigations.
The European Center for Disease Prevention and Prevention (ECDC) establishes that, although it is not entirely clear how easily the disease spreads, a person usually infects from two to three others. The virus survives from hours to days on surfaces.
Specifically, it was discovered that the virus is detectable for up to three days in plastic (polypropylene) and 304 stainless steel for one day in cardboard and for for up to four hours in copper.
This, however, varies according to humidity and temperature. Pets and other animals have already tested positive for COVID-19.
There’s no piece of evidence that pets can pass the virus to humans. However, British authorities are advising to wash their hands after contact with animals, as well as after contact with surfaces that infected people may have touched.
Coronavirus of Severe Acute Respiratory Syndrome (SARS-CoV-2) is a new virus, isolated for the first time from three persons with pneumonia connected to a set of cases of acute respiratory disease in Wuhan.
All the characteristics of the new SARS-CoV-2 virus are similar to related coronaviruses in its nature. Outside the human body, the virus dies of homemade soap, which dissolves its protective envelope. SARS-CoV-2 is closely associated with the original SARS-CoV.
It is believed to have a zoonotic origin.
The genetic analysis revealed that the coronavirus is genetically associated with Betacoronavirus, subgenus Sarbecovirus (Clinage B).Two strains of this same lineage are derived from morceats.
It bears 96% resemblance, throughout the genome, to other coronavirus samples from pustoms (BatCoCoV RaTG13).
In February of 2020, Chinese researchers discovered that there is only one amino acid of difference on certain parts of the genome-sequences between pangolin viruses and humans.
Comparating the entire genome, so far, has found at a maximum of 92% of sharing of genetic material between pangolin coronavirus and SARS-CoV-CoV-2, which is insufficient to prove that pangolins are the intermediate host.
Infection with the virus can be tentatively diagnosed based on the symptoms. The definitive confirmation, however, is by the reverse transcription followed by the polymerase (rRT-PCR) chain reaction of infected registries or computed tomography (TCT).
A study done in Wuhan, compassing rRT-PCR at CT, suggested that T (TCT it is significantly more sensitive, though it is less specific, with many of its imaging characteristics coinciding with other pneuzones and pathological processes.
As of March 2020, The American College of Radiology has recommended that "CT not be used for screening or as a front-line test for COVID-19 diagnosis."
WHO has published several RNA-regulatory RNA protocols for SARS-CoV-2, the first of which has been published on January 17.
The test uses reversive transcription followed by reaction and polymerase (rRT-PCR) in real-time.
The test can be done in respiratory or blood samples.
Results are usually available from a few hours to days.
Usually, this test is performed on nasopharyngeal smeasury smeasury, although throat smear is also used. Several laboratories and companies are developing serological tests capable of detecting antibodies.
By April 6, 2020, none of these had proved sufficiently accurate to be approved for widespread use.
A serological test developed by Cellex in the United States was approved to be used on an emergency basis only by certified laboratories.
The particularities of radiograph images and computed tomography (Computer CT) of symptomatic people include asynmetric peripheral matte glass opacities and absence of pleural eflasps.
“The Italian Radiological Society is putting together an international online database of imaging results in confirmed cases.
Due to overlaps with other infections such as adenovirus, unconfirmed CRP images are of limited specificity when identifying COVID-19.
A large study in China has likened thoracic CRI CT results and demonstrated that although the image is less specific for the infection, it is quicker and more sensitive, which suggests its consideration as an investigative tool in epidemic areas.
Convolutional neural networks based on artificial intelligence have been developed for the detection of characteristics of virus images with radiography and with TW.
Strategies to edify the transmission of the disease include maintaining a good personal hygiene in general, washing hands, avoiding touching the eyes, nose or mouth without washing your hands, sneering or sneezing on a tissue and putting this handkerchief directly into a waste container.
It is advisable who has already been infected to wear a surgical mask in public.
Physical detachment measures are also recommended to avoid transmission. Many governments have been restricting or inadvising unessential travel going to or from countries and areas affected by the outbreak.
However, the virus has reached the stage of community propagation in vast regions of the world.
This means that the virus is spreading within communities and that some members of the community do not know where or how they were infected. It is recommended for health professionals who take care of individuals who may be infected using standard precautions, contact precautions and oblical protection. Contact tracing is an important method for health authorities to determine the source of an infection and to prevent further transmission.
The use by governments of localization data from cell phones for this purpose has thlicited privacy issues, with Amnesty International and more than 100 other organizations issuing a statement asking for limits on this type of vigilance.
A number of mobile apps have been implemented or proposed for voluntary use. On April 7, 2020, more than a dozen groups of specialists are working on privacy-amenable solutions, such as using Bluetooth to record a user's proximity to other mobile phones.
The users then receive a message if they have been in close contact with someone who tested positive for COVID-19. Some misconcepts about how to prevent the infection have circulated, such as rinsing their nose and gargling with collomatories, which has already been proven in ineffective.
There's no COVID-19 Vaccination. However, several organizations are working to develop one.
Washing your hands is recommended to prevent the spread of the disease.
The CDC recommends that people wash their hands often with water and soap for at at least twenty seconds, especially after using the toilet or when their hands are visibly dirty. You should also wash their hands before eating and after blowing the nose, coughing or sneezing.
That's because, outside the human body, the virus is killed by homemade soap, which ruptures its protective cap.
The CDC further recommends the use of alcohol-based hand sanitizer with a minimum volume of at least 60% of alcohol when there is no soap and water readily available.
The WHO urges to avoid tapping the eyes, nose or mouth without rinsing your hands.
Settlements can be decontaminated with various solutions (withwithin up to one-hundred-minute exposure to the disinfectant for a stainless-steel surface), including 62–71% ethanol, isopropyl alcohol at 50–100%, 0.1% sodium hypochlorite, 0.5% hydrogen perxioxide and yodopovidone at 0,2–3.
Other solutions such as benzalcionium chloride and chlorhexidine gluconate are less effective.
The CDC recommends that in the case of suspicion or COVID-19 confirmation in one location, such as an office or a day care center, all areas such as offices, toilets, common areas, shared electronic equipment such as tablets, touchscreens, keyboards, remote controls, and ATMs used by sick people should be disinfected.
Health organizations recommend covering the mouth and nose with the elbow folded or with a handker’s handkerchief while coughing or sneezing, and immediately discarding any handkerchief.
Surgical masks are recommended for those that may be infected, since wearing a masquered mask may limit the volume and distance displacement of expiratory dropbladder when speaking, sneezing, or cough.
WHO has published instructions on when and how to wear masks.
According to Stephen Griffin, a Virologist at the University of Leeds, "Mask wear can reduce people's propensity to touch their faces, which is one of the greatest sources of infection without proper hand hygiene." It's also been recommended the use of masks by caregivers of people who may have the disease.
The WHO has recommended masking use by healthy people only when they’re at a high-promoting risk, such as carers for COVID-19 people, while also acknowledging that wearing masks may help people not touch their own faces.
In a number of countries, the use of face masks by the general public has begun to be encouraged.
In the U.S., the CDC recommend the use of non-medicinal masking for the face made of sloop. China specifically recommended the use of disposable medical masquerade by healthy people in general, especially when coming in close contact with another person (stay 1 meter or less away).
In Hong Kong it is recommended to wear a surgical mask when using public transport or when remaining in crowded places.
Thailand's Sanitary Authorities are encouraging people to do cloth face masks at home and wash them on a daily basis.
In the Czech Republic and Slovak Slovakia, it is forbidden to go out in public without wearing a mask or covering the nose and mouth.
On March 16, Vietnam requested that they all wear a face mask while leaving in public areas in order to protect themselves and others.
Austria’s government has called for everyone who enter supermarkets should wear a face mask.
In Israel, all residents were asked to wear masks for their faces in public.
Taiwan, which has been producing ten million masks a day since mid-March, required passengers on inter-municipal trains and buses to wear face masks from April 1.
In Panama it is obligatory the use of mask for the face when coming out on the street. At the same time it was recommended the production of masquerade masks at home for those who are unable to buy.
Masks for their faces have also been extensively used in Japan, South Korea, Malaysia, and Singapore.
Social detachment (also known as physical distancing) involves infection control actions aimed at decelerating transmission of the disease by reducing close contact between individuals.
Methories include quarantines, travel restrictions and the closure of schools, workplaces, stadiums, theaters or shopping malls.
Social distancing methods can be applied by all when staying at home, limiting their trips, avoiding crowded areas, using non-contained salutes and keeping distance from others.
Right now, many governments are obligating or advocating social detachment in epidemic-affected regions.
The maximum number of people gathered recommended by U.S. government agencies and healthcare organizations has been quickly reduced by 250 people (if there is no knowledge of COVID-19 transmission in one region) to 50 people and later for 10 people.
On March 22, 2020, Germany forbade public gatherings of more than two people. Older adults and those with underlying ailments such as Diabetes, Heart Disease, Respiratory Disease, Hyper-Horth and Complied Immunological Systems face a greater risk of Serious Diseases and Complicated by the CDC to Stay at Home as Much as Possible in Community Epidehyderm areas. At the end of March 2020, the WHO and other health agencies have begun to replace the use of the term "de-dispensation".
The use of the term "social insistancing" led to the implications that social isolation was needed altogether rather than encouraging contact by other alternative means. Some authorities published sexual health guidelines during the Panhode.
These recommendations include having sex only with someone you live with who doesn't have the virus or virus symptoms.
Self-isolating at home was recommended to those who were diagnosed with COVID-19 and for those who suspect they were infected.
The health agencies issued detailed instructions for proper self-isolation.Many governments have required or recommended self-quarantining for entire populations living in affected areas.
The more incisive self-quarantine instructions were evoked for those in high-fescule groups.
It was advised of individuals who may have been exposed to others with COVID-19 and those who have recently traveled to a country or region with large-scale transmission that perform 14 days of self-quarantining from the time of the last exposure possible.
Strategies to control an outburglicment are restraint or suppression and reduction.
Containment takes place in the preliminary stages of an off-exturgence and is aimed at tracking and isolating the infected, as well as presenting other infection control measures and vaccines that prevent the spread of the disease to the rest of the population.
By containing the propagation of the disease is no longer possible any longer, efforts are being made in the direction of reduction: the measures taken now serve to reduce the speed of propagation and to reduce its adverse effects on the health system as well as on the population.
Containment and mitigation measures can be taken at the same time in combination.
Suppression demands more extreme measures in the sense of reversing the pandemic to a basic reproduction rate lower than 1. The management of an outbreak of infectious disease, in part, has to do with trying to reduce the peak of the epidemic.
Thus, it reduces the risk of overburdenment of health services, giving more time for vaccines and treatments to be developed.
Interventions that are not of a pharmaceutical nature and that can administer the outbreak include personal preventive measures, such as the sanitization of the hands, the use of masks for the face and self-quarantine; community measures aimed at physical distancing, such as the closure of schools and the cancellation of events that lead to agglomerations; Community involvement, in order to stimulate the acceptance and participation in these interventions; and environmental measures such as the cleanseing of the surfaces of contact.
Other countries have taken miscellaneous measures to make the spread of the virus limited.
South Korea, for example, has exhibited mass sorting and localized quarantine. In addition, the government has issued warnings about the movement of infected people.
In Singapore, those infected were given financial support when they carried out self-quarantining, and for those they did not, severe fines were imposed.
In Taiwan there was an increase in the production of masquerade masks for the face and those that stored medical supplies were punished. Simulation conducted in the United Kingdom and the United States show that the reduction (decreased rate of contagion without stopping the propagation of the epidemic) and suppression (i.e. reversal of the growth of the epidemic) present great challenges.
Optimal reduction policies can reduce the spike in demand for health services by 2/3 and deaths in half. Nevertheless, this would result in hundreds of thousands of deaths, at the same time overwhelming the health systems.
Suppression can be the best alternative, but it needs to be maintained as long as there is the circulation of the virus by the human population or until there is the availability of a vaccine, in the event that this occurs before.
Interaction in the long term in order to suppress the pandemic brings social and economic costs.
