In Australia, the Pandemic has provided a new opportunity for Chinese shoppers to sell Australian goods in China.
The activity has created a shortage of formulas for babies in some supermarkets and was subsequently banned by the Australian government.Despite the high prevalence of COVID-19 cases in northern Italy and the Wuhan region, and the resulting high demand for food products, both areas were spared an acute food shortage.
Measures adopted by China and Italy against the illegal storage and trade of essential products have been successful, adveraging acute food scarcity that has been envisaged in Europe, as well as in North America.
Northern Italy with its significant agricultural production did not notice a large reduction, but prices can increase according to industry representatives.
Empty food shelves have been found only temporarily even in the city of Wuhan, and Chinese government officials have released pork reserves to ensure sufficient food.
Similar laws do not exist in Italy, requiring food growers to maintain reservations for such emergencies.
Damage to the global economy was felt in China: According to a media report on March 16, the economy in China was hit badly in the first two months of 2020 due to measures taken by the government to shorten the spread of the virus, and retail sales fell 20.5 percent.
Because mainland China is a large economy and manufacturing center, the viral outbreak has been seen as a major destabilizing threat to the global economy.
Agathe Demarais from The Economist's intelligence unit has predicted that the markets will remain volatile until a clearer picture arises about possible outcomes.
In January 2020, some analysts estimated that the economic repercussions of an epidemic on global growth could outweigh the SARS outbreak from 2002-2004.
An estimate by an expert at the University of Washington at St. Louis indicated an Impact of $300 Billion on the World's Supply Supplies That Could Last Up to Two Years.
The Organization of the Petroleum Exporting Countries (OPEC) purportedly "confused itself" after a sharp decline in oil prices due to low demand from China.
Global equities markets fell on February 24 due to a significant increase in COVID-19 cases outside Main Mainland China.
On Feb. 27, owing growing concerns about the Coronavirus outbreak, several United States Stock Stock Exchange Indices including NASDAQ-100, S&P 500, and Dow Jones’ industry average demonstrated its most significant declines since 2008, with Dow falling at 1,191.
All the other three instalments ended the week with a decrease greater than 10%.
On February 28, Scope Ratings GmbH affirmed China's sovereign credit rating, but maintained a negative outlook.
Estimates have fallen again based on concerns about the coronavirus, the biggest fall on March 16.
