Epidemic has coincided with Chunyun, a large travel season associated with the Chinese New Year holiday.
A number of events involving huge crowds have been canceled by national and regional governments, including annual New Year's festivals, with private companies also internally shutting down their shops and tourist attractions such as Disneyland in Hong Kong and Shanghai.
Several Lunar New Year events and tourist attractions have been closed to avoid mass agglomerations, including the Forbidden City in Beijing and traditional temple events.
In 24 of China’s 31 prefectural provinces, municipalities and regions, authorities have extended the New Year’s holiday to Feb. 10, instructing most workplaces not to re-cleave.
These regions accounted for 80 percent of the country’s GDP and 90 per cent of exports.
Hong Kong has increased its level of response to infectious disease to the highest level and declared an emergency, closing schools by March and cancelling its New Year's Eve celebrations. The retail sector has been affected globally, with reductions in store times or temporary shut-ups.
Reseller visits in Europe and Latin America fell by 40 per cent.
Resellers from North America and the Mid-Middle East observed a 50–60% drop.
This has also resulted in a 33–4 percent drop in pedestrian traffic to shopping malls in March compared to February.
Shopping center operators around the world have imposed additional measures, such as greater clean-cleaning, installation of thermal scanners to verify the temperature of buyers, and the cancellation of events.According to the United Nations Economics Commission for Latin America, it is estimated that pandemic-inducduced recession could leave between 14 and 22 million more people in extreme poverty in Latin America than it would occur in situations without the pandemic.
By January and February 2020, during the peak of the epidemic in Wuhan, about 5 million people in China lost their jobs.
Many of China's 300-billion migrant farm workers have been stranded at home in in-country provinces or imprisoned in Hubei province. In March 2020, more than 10 million Americans have lost their jobs and applied for government aid.
Coronavirus outbreak could cost 47 million jobs in the United States and the unemployment rate may reach 32%, according to estimates from the Federal Reserve Bank of St. Louis. Confinement in India left tens of millions of migrary Indian workers (who are paid through daily compensation). An Angus Reid Institute survey found that 44% of Canadian families experienced some kind of unemployment. Almost 900,000 workers have lost their jobs in Spain.
