Germany’s reduced-duty pay program has been adopted by France and Great Britain.
The sectors of performance art and cultural heritage have been profoundly affected by the pandemic, affecting the operations of the organizations, as well as the people - both employed and self-employed - worldwide.
Organizations of the arts and culture sector have tried to maintain their (often publicly funded) quest to provide access to cultural heritage for the community, to maintain the safety of their employees and the public, and to help artists when possible.
In March of 2020, around the world and at various levels museums, bookstores, performance venues, and other cultural institutions were closed indefinitely with their exhibitions, events and performances canceled or demented.
In response, intensive efforts occurred to provide alternative services through digital platforms. Another recent repercussion of the disease is the cancellation of religious services, major sporting events, and other social events, such as music and concert festivals, technological conferences and fashion events.
The film sector also experienced an outcry. The Vatican announced that the ri-rites of Holy Week in Rome that would take place during the last week of the Christian Lenten period, had been canceled.
Many dioceses have urged elderly Christians to stay at home rather than to participate in Masses on Sundays; some churches have provided church services via radio, live streaming online or on television while others offer drive-in-style services.
With the Catholic Diocese of Rome shutting down its churches and chapels and empty St. Peter's square without Christian pilgrims, other religious bodies have also canceled services and limited public agglomerations in churches, mosques, synagogues, temples and gurdwaras.
