We meet under unusual circumstances this month.
COVID-19 is something that makes clear our global human interconnection and the responsibility we have towards each other.
Its challenges have no precedent, but we do know that our best actions go hand-in-gain with global empathy, co-co-co-co-operation and the creation of communities.
Companionism and the concern we’ve seen among all of our colleagues through e-mails, calls, and conversations is a not reprisable validation of the incredible human beings we have a privilege to work with.
“I’m immensely grateful and proud to have all of you like co-workers.
This past week, someone shared with me their acknowledgment for our work.
This person reminds me of how meaningful it is for the world to be able to access Wikipedia right now, and just how much a powerful symbol this essential feature remains on-line and available to everyone.
Your work makes it possible, because you keep jobs active, pay our co-workers and maintain the safety of our communities.
“The World Needs The Information That Wikipedia Provides More than Ever.
“That’s a time when not just what we do, but the way we do, it will have a significant impact on the world.
Due to the importance of this quest and your role in it, we will make important adjustments to the way we work together, starting next week.
Adjusts to Our Work and Hourlys
As Robyn previously mentioned, Team C met last night to discuss our approach and schedules for the coming days and months.
In this meeting, we consider what we think is an action appropriate to what we are experiencing and the best way to maintain sustainable organization during this period.
“We want to end the stress and support our long-term quest.
Even if you need to be away for a while, it’s no problem.
For all employees, service providers and contract workers:
our expectation of daily work will be 4 hours, or 20 hours per week, until it is disposed to the contrary.
We aren’t declaring a holiday. If you manage to be able to work on normal time, it could be useful for our mission.
However, the world is unpredictable right now, and if you need to take care of loved ones, buy groceries or go to the doctor, your well-being is our priority.
We don’t monitor their schedules.
If he's ill, don't work.
That rule wouldn't even need to be expressed, but we've decided to pronounce it.
You don’t have to post or paid time off. Just inform your manager to help your team review the calendar and times to make sure the key areas of the job are met.
(If you’re diagnosed COVID-19, inform Bryan of training and certification so that his industry can assist you and ensure that your situation receives the appropriate management’s attention.
Persons who work by the hour will receive full payment.
We’ve already said this before, and we’re committing to honoring our commitment to our contractors and employees who work for the hour.
Everyone will receive their salaries on the basis of their expedient normal work under normal circumstances.
Wages don’t depend on whether you’re sick and unable to work.
If you’d like to work, we fully support you.
A lot of people see work as a way to channel stress due to today’s situation in the world.
“Whether the job we do can be incredibly rewarding, especially in times like these.
Remind them that their well-being is more important.
We only ask that you communicate to your manager so that we know what to look forward to and can help activities appropriately.
Some of these functions are considered essential.
There are activities we must continue to perform.
Site Reliability, HR, Reliability, and Fundraising and Fundraising Engineering (between) teams carry out essential activities that may require additional support.
"We'll begin a process with every department to assess current goals and change our focus to support what's essential to our quest.
There’s so much to be done for all of us, and let’s focus our efforts on the most end-to-essential projects.
Scaling now won't hurt us in the future.
We don't have the intention to "work bent to make up for lost time" after the pandemic is over.
It will not be expected that you will work overtime to fulfill deadlines that are now impracticable.
“We accept that the circumstances have changed, and we will work to define new goals and deadlines as appropriate.
What Will Happen for Annual Planning?
In order to adjust to our new reality and expectations of hours worked per day, we intend to adjust the schedule for the delivery of our 2020-2021 Annual Plan.
Our intention is to propose an extension of our 2019-2020 plan, which will provide a greater time for the budget, and for employees to prioritise the essential work, personal care and the care of loved ones while settling those who need or want to work on a reduced journey in the coming weeks.
Timeline extension broadly lowers current planning workloads and pressure across the organization.
We’ll present our proposal to the board next week, and we’ll update representatives and teams on upcoming steps as soon as we get confirmation.
We are grateful for the annual planning team for their leadership in this process.
Office Status, Display & Cleaning
Over the past this past week we learned that one of our co-workers from San Francisco may have been exposed to COVID-19 virus.
In view of this, as a precaution, we employ an anti-viral cleaning team to disinfect all surfaces in San Francisco office.
They have used a special anti-viral solution for hospitals to disinfecte all surfaces, as well as the entrance hall and all the elevators giving access to our floor.
“The building is employing its own care-duty protocol using products that are secure for its attendees.
Relieved by the fact that the office will be properly prepared for when we decide to return.
Our Washington office is located on a WeWork, which has shared its COVID-19 protocol with us and with all Washington officials.
Over the past week, our office in Washington has switched to an all-remogue setup, in line with San Francisco.
As some of our co-o colleagues in New York know, we're also arguing about renting an office in Brooklyn.
Such discussions continue to take place, but they may be delayed.
Some of our colleagues are working remotely for the first time.
Colleague-workers for a long time know that it can be an adjustment, and they’d like to offer a few pieces of advice:
Please limit the duration of meetings for up to one or two hours.
If longer-lasting sessions are necessary, consider splitting them into several days.
Determine the meeting, have a schedule and send in-of-prosecution materials for reading in advance.
Use videos by default with tools such as Google Docs and Zoom to facilitate real--u- time collaboration and connection.
Have a leader to facilitate meetings, someone who monitor questions in the chat and list of attendees, and someone to help take notes (or collaborative annotations).
Please e-mail the technical support in case you need comfortable headphones.
Use Your Emergency Aid to Buy Snack Snacks.
Participate from the ?remoties channel to talk to your colleagues about remote work
The HR operations team is researching ergonomics guides in the context of the webinar to assist in increasing remote work at the Foundation.
“Last last week, we have asked all receivers to help the community that would not cancel Wikimedia-supply-financed public events, such as editing marries, until WHO declares an end to the Pandemic.
“We informed them that we would understand that our application for cancellation and other restrictions could make it impossible to complete their agreed subsidy activities and that no one would be penalized for extending or modifying their goals.
Next week we’ll continue with additional Guides on WikiMania and other regional and community-themed conferences.
The general feeling in the world community seems to be one of sadness with paralysis, but also of relief with the clarity and ability to stay focused on one’s own communities, at Wikimedia and in other situations.
“The communications resource team is working on creating a page on Meta-Wiki to provide a space for the community to monitor the impact and receive our communications to it.
Staying up-to-to-date on COVID-19-Related
We'll send an invitation to their calendars for next Thursday at 14:00 UTC/ 07:00 PT for a special meeting with employees.
We'll be using that time to share additional updates, answer your questions, and spend some time connecting with each other.
We’re on this together and we’ll help with whatever it takes.
In the meantime, you can continue to find this e-mail information and all other COVID-19-related information on the company wiki.
The communication resource team will update these pages and all information in a single location.
We’re also working to maintain regular communication with employees who live in significantly affected countries today.
If you’ve got any questions about trips, events, a core workflow, or the coverage challenge, or anything else you might need for help that you may need help, be sure to notify you and work with the communication resources team.
“We’re here to help provide support and collaboration as needed.
If you have a confidential or sensitive matter, please email Bryan Judan, Global HR Chief Operating Officer.
None of these changes should be viewed as abandonment of our work and obligations.
They’re nonetheless an acknowledgment that right now our work and obligations will probably need to adapt in such a way we haven’t adapted in the past.
These are the steps that we believe is necessary as support at this time, so that we can continue to work, providing our movement the support they need and providing the world with the service it depends on.
“Our planned work will await when the time comes.
For now it's time to support each other and create space for the important work that will take place in the weeks, and probably months to come.
We need all of you for this to happen, therefore we need everyone to take care of yourself and your family so that they are in your best condition when it is needed.
Rinhale your hands and don't touch your face!
“Kaquaint communication resources team (Amanda K, Amy V, Bryan J, Doreen D, Gregory V, Jaime V, Joel L, Lynette L, Ryan and Tony S”), and the rest of the Leadership Team (Grant I, Heather W, Jaime V, Janeen U, Lisa S, Robyn A, Ryan and Toby N).
Angiotensin-conververting angiotensin-converting enzyme (ECA2) is an enzyme attached to the outer surface (llcell membranes) of the cells of the lungs, arteries, heart, rhybekistan.
ECA2 neutralizes the activity of angiotensin-conververting enzyme (ACE) by reducing the amount of angiotensin II and elevating Ang (1–7), making it a promising drug aimed at treating cardiovascular disease. ECA2 also functions as an entry point in cells for some coronaviruses.
Human version of the enzyme is often referred to as hECA2.
Angiotensin-conververting angiotensin-like enzyme is a metalloenzyme that contains zinc located on the surface of endothelial and other cells.
ECA2 protein contains a pelptidasis M2 N-terminal domain and an Amateymerous and cholectring C-terminal domain in the renal system.
ECA2 is a single-septal type 1 membrane protein, with its envircactically active domain exposed on the surface of the cells in the lungs and other tissues.
The extracellular ACE domain is cleaved from transmembrane dominance by another enzyme known as sheddase, and the resulting soluble protein is released into the bloodstream and ultimately excreted in the urine.
ECA2 is present in many organs: ECA2 is coupled to the cell membrane of the main pulmonary alveolar cells of type II, enterocyte enterocytes of the small intestine, arterial and venous endothelial cells and arteriosal smooth muscle cells in the majority of the organs.
ECA m nanam expression is also found in cerebral cortex, sturplified body, hypothalamus, and brainstem.
ECA2’s main function is to act as a counterweight for the ECA.
ACE cleavages the hormone angiotensin type I in type II angiotensin causing vasoconstriction.
ECA2 claves phenylalanine from angiotensine from angiotensin II (Asp-Arg-Val-Tyr-Ile-His-Phe) and hydrolysis in angiotensine vasodilator angiotensin (1–7),(H-Asp-Arg-Val-Tyr-Ile-Gist-Pro-OH).
ECA also clave several other squats, peptides including bradykinin, apalin, neurotensin, dinorphine, ghrelin and ghrelin.
ECA2 also regulates transport by membrane of the neutral amino acid transporte transporte transporter SCL6A19 was implicated in Hartnup disease.
Like a transmembrane protein, ECA2 functions as the main point of entry into cells for some coronavirus, including HCoV-NL63; SARS-CoV (the viruses that cause SARS); and SARS-CoV-2 (the virus that causes COVID-19).
More specifically, SARS-CoV-CoV and SARS-CoV2 splash protein binding to ACE's enzimatic domain on cell surface results in endocytosis and translocation of the virus and enzyme in endosomes located within cells.
This entry process also requires the preparation of S-protein by stewardesse-protease TM PRSS2, whose inhibition is under investigation as a potential therapy. This has led some to hypothesize that reducing ACS2 levels in the cells may help fight the infection.
However, a number of professional societies and regulators have recommended to continue with ACE-reinhibiting pattern and ARB therapy.
A systematic review and meta-analyses published on July 11, 2012 found that “the use of ACE inhibitors was associated with a Significant Reduction of Pneumonia Risk from Controls.”
In addition, "the risk of pneumonia was also reduced in patients treated with ACE suppressants who had an elevated risk of har9).
The use of ACE-inhibitors was also coupled with reduced pneumonia-Related Mortal Mortality, although the results were less sufflued than for the overall risk of pneumonia."
Recombinant human ACA (rhACE2) is believed to be a new therapy for acute lung injury and seemed to improve pulmonary haemohydrodynamics and oxygen saturation in porks with a lipopolysaccharide-inducduced acute respiratory anguish syndrome.
The half-life of rhACE2 in humans is about 10-hour and the onset of action is 30 minutes after the temporal evolution of 24-Hour (duration) effects.
Many findings suggest that rhACE2 may be a promising drug for those with intolerance to inhibitors of the classic renine-angiotensin renine-angiotensin (RAS inhibitors) or in diseases in which circulating angiotensin II is high. Infused rhACE2 has been evaluated in clinical trials for the treatment of acute respiratory anxiety syndrome.
Apps about COVID-19 are mobile software applications to assist monitoring in response to the 2019-2020 Coronavirus Pandemic, i.e. the process of identifying people ("contacts") who may have come into contact with infected individuals.
Numerous applications have been developed or proposed, with the official support of the government in certain territories and jurisdictions.
A number of frameworks to create contact monitoring applications have been developed.
“Privacy issues have been raised, particularly about systems based on tracking the geographical location of the app’s users.
Poor less intrusive alternatives include the use of Bluetooth signals to record the user’s proximity to other cell phones.
On April 10, 2020, Google and Apple jointly announced they would be part of the functionality to support such Bluetooth-based apps directly to their Android and iOS operating systems.
In China, the Chinese government, together with Alipay, has implemented an application that allows citizens to verify if they have been in contact with a COVID-19 carrier.
It's in use in more than 200 Chinese cities. In Singapore an app called TraceTogether is being used.
The application has been developed by the local IT community, released as open-source and will be ceded to the government. Macedon of the North has launched "StopKorona!", a Bluetooth-based application to track exposure to potentially infected people and offer a quick response to health officials.
The app was developed by the Ministry of Communication and Technology and the Ministry of Health.
As of April 14, 2020, the app has been awaiting approval by the Google Play Store and the Apple App.
On April 12, the government stated that the contact tracing app had been at an advanced stage of development and would be available for distribution in weeks. A similar app is planned for Ireland and France ("StopCovid").
Australia and New Zealand envisage applications based on Singapore’s TraceTogether app and BlueTrace protocol. Russia aims to implement a geo-delimiting application for COVID-19-diagnosed patients living in Moscow, designed to make sure they don’t leave the house.
Ross Anderson, Professor of Security Engineering at the University of the University of Cambridge, has enumerated a number of potential practical issues with application-based systems, including false-positives and the potential lack of effectiveness if application acceptance is only limited to a small portion of the population.
In response to concerns over the spread of false or harmful “coronavirus” apps, Apple sets limits for the types of organizations that can add coronavirus-related apps to their App Store, bordering them only on “officized” or otherwise reputable organizations.
Google and Amazon have put in place similar restrictions.
Advocaters of privacy have expressed their concern regarding the implications of mass surveillance using coronavirus applications, especially as to whether the surveillance infrastructure created to deal with the coronavirus pandemic will be dismanthelled once the threat has passed.
Amnesia Internacional and more than 100 other organizations have issued a communiqué calling for limits to this type of vigilance.
The organizations have declared eight conditions for government projects:
vigilance should be “legal, necessary and proportionate”;
extensions of monitoring and vigilance should have suspension clauses;
use of the data should be limited for COVID-19 purposes;
the security and annoyance of data should be protected and presented as protected on the basis of evidence;
digital vigilance should avoid aggravating discrimination and marginalisation;
any sharing of data with third parties should be provided for by law;
there should be harmatures against abuse and the rights of citizens to respond to abuse;
Significant participation of all "relevant interested parties" would be required, including that of public health experts and marginalized groups. The Chaos Computer Club (CCC) in Germany and Reporters Without Borders (Reporter ohne Grenzen) (RSF) have also published checklists.
The plan proposed by Google/Apple intends to deal with the persistent surveillance problem by removing the tracking mechanism of its device operating systems when it is no longer needed.
Some countries have used network-based localization tracking instead of applications, eliminating the need to download an application and the ability to avoid tracking.
Network-based tracing in Israel has been approved.
Re-accurate solutions that have access to raw localization data have sizable potential privacy issues.
However, not all systems with central servers need access to personal localization data; a range of privacy-preserving systems have been created to use core servers for intercommunication only (see section below).
In South Korea a non-in-Application-Based System Was Used to Perform Contact Tracing.
Instead of using an exclusive application, the system has gathered tracking information from a variety of sources, including mobile device-tracking data and card transaction data, combining it to generate text messages for potentially-infected individuals.
In addition to using this information to alert potential interlocutors, the government has also made localization information publicly available, which is allowed due to sweeping changes to information confidentiality laws after the MERS surge in the country.
This information is made freely available to the public through a number of applications and websites. The countries, including Germany, consider it to use both the privacy preservation system and the centralized.
Until April 6, 2020, the details had not yet been released.
Confessional tracking preserving privacy is a well-definite concept, with a significant body of scientific bibliography retracing at least 2013. Since April 7, 2020, more than a dozen expert groups have been working on privacy-respecting solutions, using Bluetooth Low Energy (BLE).
However, PEPP-PT is a co-roordinating effort that embraces centralized and de-decentralized approaches, and it is not a one-of-a-kind protocol. Decentralized privacy-Preserving Proximity Tracing (DP-PPT/DP-3T), Temporary Contact Numbers (TCN, Fka Contact Event Numbers, CEN)
According to these protocols, the personally identifiable data never leaves the device, and any combination is made on the device.
The Privacy Group at the MIT Media Lab has developed Safe pathways, a platform for the use of privacy-preservation techniques during collection and use of location data or cross-comes to track the propagation of COVID-19.
It’s based on research from the technical publication “Applications Throw Dirty: Keep Personal Privacy in an Epidemic” released in March 2020. Another similar endeavor is Enigma MPC's SafeTrace platform, an issue that develops privacy technologies and originally founded at the MIT Media Lab.
SafeTrace utilizes secure hardware technologies to enable users to share sensitive location and healthcare data with other and responsible users without jeopardizing the privacy of that data.
On April 5, 2020, the TCN Global Coalition was founded by groups that rallied around what was essentially the same approach and in large part with overlapping of protocols, with the aim of reducing fragmentation, and enabling global idly interoperability of alert and tracking applications, a key aspect of gaining widespread adoption.
On April 9, 2020, the Government of Singapore announced that BlueTrace's protocol, used by the government's official app, became open-source.
On April 10, 2020, Google and Apple, companies that control Android and iOS mobile platforms, announced an initiative for contact tracing, in which they alleged they would preserve privacy, based on a combination of Bluetooth Low Energy technology and encryption for privacy preservation.
They also published specifications of the main technologies used in the system.
According to Apple and Google, the system envisages implementation in three stages:
Developing Tools to Allow the Government To Create Official Coronavirus Tracking Apps Preserving Privacy
integration of this functionality directly in iOS and Android; Google and Apple plan to address the persistent and assumed surveillance issues by first distributing the system by operating system updates, and later removing it in the same way as soon as the threat ends.
Repositioning (also known as drug repropose, re-profiling, reastribution or therapeutic sub-replate) is the replenishment of a drug approved for the treatment of a medical condition or disease other than that for which it was originally developed.
This is one of the lines of scientific research that are currently being adopted to develop safe and effective treatments for COVID-19.
Other research targetings include the development of convalescent plasma transfusion and COVID-19-vacation. SARS-CoV-2 has about 66 doyble proteins, each with multiple binding points.
Analysing these binding points provides a reasonable project of effective antiviral drug against COVID-19 proteins.
Among SARS-CoV-2's single-targeting proteins include papain-like protease, rRNA polymerase RNA, helicase, Protein S and ADP ribofosphatase.
Hussein A. A., et al., studied several candidate compounds, which he then perfected and analysed for his structural similarities with the most similar approved drugs in order to accelerate the development of a steril potent drug against SARS-CoV-2 in his pre-clinical study to be recommended for a conception of clinical study.
chloroquine is a medicinal drug against malaria which is also used against some autoimmune diseases.
On March 18, the WHO announced chloroquine and hydrohydrochloroquine-Related would be among the four drugs studied as part of the Solidarity clinical trial.
New York Governor Andrew Cuomo has already announced that tests with chloroquine and Hydroxychloroquine in New York would begin on March 24. On March 28 the FDA authorized the use of hydroxy-chlorochloroquine sulfate and chloroquine phosphate through an Emergency Use Authorization (AUE).
The treatment has not been endorsed by the process of clinical trials of the FDA and is authorized through A UUE only as an experimental treatment for emergency use in hospitalized patients who cannot receive treatment in a clinical trial.
The CDC has said "the use, dosage, or duration of hydro hydrochloroquin for prophylaxis or treatment of SARS-CoV-CoV-2 infection" has not yet been established.
Doctors have said they are using the drug when “there’s no other option.”
In Istanbul, a Turkish research team is conducting a small research study on chloroquine use in combination with Zinc, Vitamin A, Vitamin C, and Vitamin D.
Exceeding large studies at Duke University and the University of Oxford.
NYU’s Langone School of Medicine is conducting an assay on the Safety and Effectiveness of Hydroxychloroquine Preventive Use.
Chinese Clinical Trials in Wuhan and Shenzhen claimed to demonstrate that favipiravir was "clearly effective".
In Shenzhen, 35 patients tested negative within 4 days on an on average, while the duration of the disease in the 45 unreserved patients was 11 days.
In a study conducted in Wuhan with 240 patients with phanu pneumonia, half were treated with favipiravir, and half received umifenovir.
The Italian Pharmaceutical Agency reminded the population that the existing evidence to support the drug is scarce and preliminary.
On April 2, Germany advertised that it would purchase Japan's drug to stolocate it and would use the army to deliver it to university hospitals, where it would be used to treat COVID-19 patients.
According to The South China Morning Post, Shinzo Abe has been creating openings for the Trump administration on the purchase of the drug. The drug can be less effective in severe cases of the disease, when the virus has already multiplied.
Their use may not be unsecured for expectant mothers or women trying to get pregnant.
A Lopinavir/ritonavir (Kaletra) study, a combination of lopinavir antiviers and ritonavir, concluded that "no benefit has been observed".
Dropsies have been developed to inhibit the replication of HIV by binding to protease.
A team of researchers at the University of Colorado is trying to modify the drugs to find a compound that is linked to SARS-CoV-2 protease. There are criticisms in the scientific community about directing resources to repropose drugs developed specifically for HIV/AIDS.
WHO included lopinavir/ritonavir in the International Essay Solidarity.
Remdesivir was created and developed by Gilead Sciences as a treatment for Ebola virus disease and Marburg virus infections. Gilead Sciences later discovered that remdesivir had anti-viral activity in vitro against phylum, pneumo, parabirmo and coronavirus.
One problem with anti-viral treatment is resistance developed by mutations, which can cause more severe transmissions or diseases.
Initial pre-rehearsal studies suggest that remdesivivir may have a high genetic barrier to resistance. There are a number of ongoing clinical trials, including two conducted by Cleveland University Hospitals, one for people with moderated disease and one for those with more serious disease.
There have been three clinical trials with intravenous vitamin C taking place for people who are hospitally hospitalized and severely ill with COVID-19: two of them are controlled by placebo (in China and Canada), while the other does not have a control group (Italy).
The State of New York began to carry out tests with azithromycin an antibiotic on March 24, 2020.
Japan’s National Center for Health and Global Medicine (NCGM) is planning a clinical trial of Alvesco (ciclesonide), from Teijin, which is an inhalable corticosteroid for asthma. The goal is the treatment of pre-symptomatic patients infected with new coronavirus.
Phase II of a study with an angiotensin-conververting angiotensin 2 is underway with 200 patients, to be recruited among severe cases hospitalized in Denmark, Germany, and Austria to determine the effectiveness of the treatment.
At the moment, researchers from the Heart Institute in Canada are researching colchicine’s role in reducing pulmonary complications and inflammation in patients with mild COVID-19 symptoms.
The study is recruiting 6,000 adults over the age of 40, diagnosed with COVID-19 and with mild symptoms that did not need to be hospitalized.
Prefectural, breast-breasted women who are breast-floating or who do not use effective contraceptive methods have not been included.
Several anticoagulants are being tested in Italy.
Heparin of low molecular weight is being used extensively used in the treatment of patients, which has led the Italian Medicines Agency to publish guidance on use.
A multi-center study of 300 patients is looking at the use of enoxaparin sodium in prophylaxis and therapeutic dosing. The study was announced on April 14 in Italy.
Because SARS-CoV-CoV-2 is a virus, the attention of the scientific community has been considerable in the sense of giving new purpose to antiviral drugs approved and developed for the treatment of previous outbreaks such as MERS, SARS and Western Nile viruses.
Ribavirin: ribavirin was recommended to treat COVID-19, according to the 7th edition of Chinese guidelines
Umifenovir: umifenovir has been recommended for COVID-19 treatment, according to the 7th edition of the Chinese guidelines
Some antibiotics have been identified to receive a new purpose for the treatment of COVID-19 patients:
Tocilizumab(anti-IL-6 receiver): Approved in China
There will also be tests in Italy and in China. See Tocilizumab-COVID-19.
COVID-19 Vaccination is a hypothetical coronavirus (COVID-19) vaccine against coronavirus (COVID-19).
While there’s no vaccine with completed clinical trials, there are a number of ongoing attempts to steward them.
In late February 2020, the World Health Organization (WHO) stated that it does not expect a vaccine against SARS-CoV-2, a virus that causes the disease, available in less than 18 months.
Five Vaccination Candidates Were in Phase I of Safety Studies in April.
COVID-19 was identified in December 2019.
A major spread of the outbreak took place in 2020, which has led to considerable investments and research activities for the development of a vaccine.
A number of organizations are using published genomes for the development of possible SARS-CoV-CoV-2 vaccines.
CEPI announced in April that the needs for vaccine development speed, manufacturing capability, large-scale development, large-scale development and global access.
In April CEPI scientists reported 10 diverse technology platforms being surveyed and developing during early 2020 to create an effective COVID-19 vaccine.
Goals from the larger platforms that have moved towards phase I security studies include:
Nucleic Acid (DNA or RNA) (Moverevolent in Phase I and Vaccine Applicant: Modern, m RNA-1273)
viral vector (Phase I stevolveter and vaccine candidate: CanSino Biologics, adenovirus type 5 vector)
Reported by CEPI scientists in April, a total of 115 Vaccine Candidates are in the early stages of development, with 78 of them confirmed as active projects (79, According to the Milken Institute). Another 37 have been announced, but with little information available to the public (considered in planning or being projected).
A Phase I-II test performs safety testing and primary immunogenicity. It’s typically randomized, placebo-controlled and multicentre at the same time that it determines more precise and effective dosses.
Phase III trials typically involve more participants in a control group. They test the vaccine’s efficacy in preventing the disease, while at the same time they monitor adverse effects at the optimal dose.
Of the 79 vaccine applicants in active-developing (confirmed at the beginning of April of 2020), 74 did not yet have an evaluation in humans (they follow in preclinical research).
On January 24, 2020, the University of Queensland announced that it is investigating the potential of a molecular staple vaccine, which would genetically modify viral proteins to stimulate an immune reaction.
On January 24, 2020, in Canada, the International Vaccination Center (VIDO-InterVac) at the University of Saskatchewan Announces Start of Work in a Vaccine with the goal of testing in humans in 2021.
Vaccine development projects have been announced at China’s Center for Disease Prevention and Control on Jan. 26, 2020.
On January 29, 2020, the Janssen Pharmaceutical Companies, led by Hanneke Schuitemaker, announced the beginning of their work for developing a vaccine.
Janssen co-developing an Oral Vaccination With its Biotechnology Partner Vaxart.
On March 18, 2020, Emergent BioSolutions announced an manufacturing partnership with Vaxart to develop the vaccine.
On February 8, 2020, OncoGen Laboratory in Romania published a document on the elaboration of a vaccine with technology similar to that used for neo-antigen vaccination therapy.
On March 25, the head of the research institute announced they had finalized synthesis of the vaccine and were initiating tests.
On Feb. 27, 2020, a subsidiary of Generex, NuGenerex Immnuno-Oncology, announced that they were initiating a Vaccination Project to Create a COVID-19 Li-Key Pepty Vaccination.
“They wanted to produce a vaccine candidate that could be tested in humans “in the next 90 days.”
On March 5, 2020, the University of Washington in St. Louis announced its plans to develop a vaccine.
On March 5, 2020, United States Army Medical Research and Development Research and Development at Fort Detrick and the Walter Reed Army Research Research Institute in Silver Spring, both in the western region of Maryland, announced that they were working on a vaccine.
By March 10, 2020, Emergent Biosolutions announced it has merged to Novavax Inc.
in the development and manufacture of a vaccine.
The female partners subsequently announced plans for pre-clinical testing and Phase I of clinical studies in July 2020.
On March 12, 2020, India’s Ministry of Health announced that they are working with 11 isolates and, even at an expedited, would take at at least one-and-a-a-half years to develop a vaccination.
On March 12, 2020, Medicago, a biotechnology company in the city of Quebec, rediscovered the development of a Coronavirus-like particle under partial funding of the Canadian Institutes for Health Research.
Vaccine candidate is under research in the laboratory, with human trials planned for July or August 2020.
Earlier this week, The Guardian stated that U.S. President Donald Trump offered CureVac "large sums of money for exclusive access to Covid-19 vaccine" under protest from the German government.
On March 17, 2020, pharmaceutical company Pfizer announced a partnership with German company BioNTech to jointly develop an mRNA-Based Vaccine.
BNT162, a candidate MRNA-based vaccine, currently in pre-clinical testing, with clinical trials expected to begin in April 2020.
In Italy on March 17, 2020, Takis Biotech, an Italian bio-bio-technology company announced that it would have pre-clinical test results in April 2020 and its ultimate vaccine candidate could depart for human trials in the fall.
On March 19, 2020, Co-alk of Innovation in Epidemic Preparation (CEPI) announced an investment of US$4.9 million in an COVID-19 vaccine research consortium involving Institut Pasteur, Themis Bioscience (Vienna, Austria), and the University of Pittsburgh, taking CEPI’s total investment in the COVID-19 vaccine’s development for COVID to US$29 million.
CEPI’s other investment partners for vaccine development for COVID-19 are Moderna, Curevac, Inovio, Novavax, The University of Hong Kong, the University of Oxford, and the University of Queensland.
On March 20, 2020, Russian health officials announced that scientists had begun testing animals for six different vaccine candidates.
Researchers from Imperial College in London announced on March 20, 2020 that they are developing an RNA self-amplifying vaccine for COVID-19.
Vaccination mayor has been developed within 14 days of receiving sequencing from China.
At the end of March, the Canadian government announced C$275 million in funding for 96 clinical counter-meathese projects against COVID-19, including several Vaccine Candidates at Canadian companies and universities such as Medicago and the University of Saskatchewan initiatives.
In almost the same period, the Canadian government announced $192 million specifically to develop the COVID--19 vaccine, with plans to establish a national “vaccination bench” of several new vaccines that could be used if other Coronavirus outbreaks occur.
On April 2, 2020, researchers at the University of Pittsburgh School of Medicine reported the PittCoVacc trial, a possible COVID-19 vaccine in mice, stating that "MNA has delivered SARS-CoV-CoV-2 sub-university vaccines that aroused ug potent antibogen-specific anti-antiboit [in the mouse] antigens [in the rat] that were evident that began 2 weeks after immunization."
In Canada on April 16, 2020, Waterloo's School of Pharmacy University announced the elaboration of a DNA-Based Vaccine Applicant as possibly a Nasal Spray.
Using bacterióphages, the DNA will be designed to replicate itself inside a human bacterium to produce non-offensive particles similar to the virus, which can stimulate the immune system to produce antibodies against SARS-CoV-2.
In March of 2020, the government, industry, and three U.S. universities gathered resources to access IBM supercomputers in combination with cloud computing from  Hewlett Packard Enterprise of Amazon, Microsoft and Google.
Some vaccines have heteroloneurological effects, also called non-specific effects.
That means they may have benefits beyond the disease it prevents.
A subsequent randomized trial in Australia is seeking to register 4,170 health workers.
It’s possible that in developing vaccines aren’t safe or effective.
Inadequate research to evaluate the effectiveness of the vaccine using specimens of animals specific to COVID-19, such as ACE2-transgenic rats, other laboratory animals and non-human primates, indicate a need for containment measures for tier 3 biosafety to manipulate living viruses, and an international co-occupature to ensure standardized safety procedures.
Vaccines against SARS and MERS have already been tested in non-human animal specimens.
In 2020, there’s no cure or vaccine that protects against SARS that’s proven safe and effective in humans.
Based on research documents published in 2005 and 2006, the identification and development of new vaccines and medications to treat SARS was a priority for governments and public health agencies around the world. There is also no proven vaccine against MERS.
When MERS became predominant, it was believed that existing SARS research could provide a useful model for the development of Vaccines and therapeutic treatment against MERS-CoV infection.
Starting in March 2020, there was a vaccine against MERS (DNA-Based-Based) that completed Phase I Clinical Trials in Humans, and three others in progress, all of which are virus-vetroduced vaccines, two with an adenoviral vector (ChAdOx1-MERS, B-VRS-GamVac), and one MVA (MVA-MER-S).
Social media publications promoted a conspiracy theory claiming that the virus behind COVID-19 was known and that a vaccine was already available.
The patents cited by various publications on social media indicate existing patents for genetic sequences and vaccines for other Coronavirus strains such as Coronavirus SARS.
Beta Coronavirus (COVID-19) is an infectious disease caused by an acute acute respiratory syndrome of Coronavirus 2 (SARS-CoV-2).
Common symptoms include fever, a tweezing, and difficulty breathing.
Other symptoms might include fatigue, muscle pain, diarrhea, sore throat, loss of smell and abdominal pain.
Exposure to the onset of symptoms is about 5 days, but can vary from two to fourteen days.
Although the majority of cases results in mild symptoms, some progress toward viral pneumonia and multiple organ failure.
As of April 17, 2020, over 2.24 million had been reported in 210 countries and territories, resulting in over 153,000 deaths.
More than 5,68,000 people have been cursed. The virus is disseminated among people mainly during direct contact, usually through droplets produced by cough, sneezing, or speech.
Although these droplets are produced when exhaling, they typically fall to the ground or stay on surfaces instead of remaining infectious at long distances.
People can also become infected by touching contaminated surfaces and then touch the eyes, nose or mouth.
The virus can survive on surfaces for up to 72 hours.
It is contagious during the first 3 days after the onset of the symptoms, although the spread may be possible before symptoms appear and in later stages of the disease. The standard method of diagnosis is by polymeramerase chain reaction of real-time retranscription (rRT-PCR) of a nasopharyngeal swab.
