Coronapt-Obisufect of Sevetal Respirary Respiratory Syndrome (SARS-CoV-2) Coronavirus is a novel coronavirus from acute respiratory syndrome that was first isolated in three people with pneumonia that were associated with a group of acute respiratory diseases.
All features of the new SARS-CoV-2 virus take place in nature, in related coronaviruses.
Outside of the human body the virus dies in contact with household soap, which disrupts its protective layer. SARS-CoV-2 is closely connected to the original SARS-CoV.
The lungs are the organs most afflicted by COVID-19 because the virus accesses the host cells through angiotensin-conververting angiotensin enzyme (ACE2), which is more abundant in alveolar cells type II of the lungs.
The virus uses special surface glycoprothecine called "spilistone" (pelometer) to connect to ACE2 and enter the host cell.
Acute cardiac injury was seen in 12% of those infected who have been admitted to the hospital in Wuhan, China, and is more frequent in severe disease.
Cardocardial symptom rate is high thanks to systemic inflammatory response and disorders of the immune system during disease progression, but acute myocardial injury may also be related to ACE2 receptors in the heart.
ACE2 receivers are highly emenvised on the heart and are engaged in cardiac function.
A high incidence of thrombosis (31%) and venous thromboembolism (25%) was found in ICU Patients with COVID-19 Infections and may be related to poor prognosis. Necropsies of people who died from COVID-19 showed fuzzy alveolar (DAD) and inflammatory infiltrates containing lymphocytes in the lung.
While SARS-CoV-CoV-2 tropism for epithelial cells of the respiratory tract that elicit ACE2, patients with severe COVID-19 present symptoms of systemic hyperinflammation.
In particular, pathogenic T-cells that secrete GM-CSF have been shown to be correlated with IL-6 secretory inflammatory monocyte recruitment and severe pulmonary pathology in COVID-19 patients.
Lymphocytic infiltrators have also been reported on necropsies.
The WHO has published a number of testing protocols for the disease.
Default testing method is polyamerase chain reaction via real-time reverse transcribing (rRT-PCR).
The test is usually done on respiratory samples obtained through an nasopharyngeal swab, however a nasal swab or phalborage sample can be used.
Pretrece is usually available within a few hours for up to two days.
Blood tests can be used, but for this it is necessary to collect two blood samples with two weeks difference and the results have little immediate value.
The Chinese scientists were able to insulate a strain of coronavirus and to publish its genetic sequence so laboratories around the world could independently develop polymerase-chain-chain (PCR (PCR) tests to detect virus infection.
As of April 4, 2020, anti-antibodi testing (which can detect active infections and whether a person has been infected in the past) were developing but have not yet been widely used.
The Chinese experiment with testing has shown that its accuracy is 60-70%.
The U.S. FDA approved the world’s first Remote Laboratory Test on March 21, 2020, for use late that month. Diagnosis guidelines released by Wuhan’s Zhongnan Hospital suggest methods for detecting infections based on clinical resources and epidemiological risk.
