Pre-presutive measures to reduce the chances of infection include staying at home, avoiding places with many people, often washing their hands with soap and water for at at least 20 seconds, practising good respiratory hygiene and avoiding touching the eyes, nozzle and mouth with hands without washing.
The CDC recommends covering your mouth and nose with a handkertif when swirling or sneezing, as well as covering one’s mouth with the inner side of the elbow if the scarf is undisclosed.
Appropriate hygienic of the hand after coughing or sneezing is encouraged.
The CDC has recommended the use of rag masks in public places, in part to limit transmission by asymptomatic individuals. Social distancing strategies are aimed at reducing the contact of infected people with large groups by shutting down schools and work places, restricting trips, and cancelling large public meetings.
Detachment guidelines also include that people stay at at least 1.8 meters (6 feet) away from each other.
It is not known an effective drug for the prevention of COVID-19. Since the vaccine should not be launched before 2021, a key point for controlling COVID-19 is to try to decrease the peak of the epidemic, also known as "curve scarring".
The CDC also recommend that individuals wash their hands frequently using soap and water for at least 20 seconds, especially after going to the toilet or when their hands are noticeably dirty, before eating and after blowing their nose, cough or sneezing.
It also recommends using alcohol-based antiseptics for hands with at least 60 percent alcohol, but only when soap and water aren’t readily available. For sites where non-commercial optimps are not readily available, the WHO provides two formulations for local production.
In these formulations, anti-microbial activity occurs through ethanol or isopropanol.
Hydrogen peroxide is used to help dispose of bacterial spores in alcohol; it’s not “an active substance for hand asepsis.”
glycerol is added as anhydrificant.
People are treated through pallipative care, which may include fluid therapy, oxygen support, and support for other affected vital organs.
The CDC recommends that people who suspect they have the virus use a simple face mask.
Oxygenation of extracorporeal Membrane Oxygenation (EMEC) has been used to treat the problem of respiratory inadequacy, but its benefits are still under analysis.
Personal hygiene and a healthy lifestyle and diet are being recommended to improve immunity.
Re-attentive treatments can be helpful for those with mild symptoms and in the early stages of infection. The WHO and the National Health Commission of China have published recommendations for the care of people hospitalized with COVID-19.
Intensivists and pulmonologists of the U.S. gathered treatment recommendations from various agencies in a free resource, the IBCC.
Until April 2020, there is no specific COVID-19 treatment.
For the symptoms, some healthcare professionals recommend paracetamol (acetaminophen) rather than ibuprofen for first-line use.
Precautions must be taken to minimise the risks of transmission of the virus, especially in the health environment, when performing procedures that can generate aerosols, such as intubation and manual ventilation.
For healthcare professionals caring for COVID-19 people, the CDC recommends placing the person in rooms with air-transmitted infections (SIIA) in addition to taking standard precautions, contact precautions and air precautions. The CDC summarizes the guidelines for the use of Personal Protective Imaging Equipment (PPE) during the Pandelight.
Recommended equipment is: PPE blanket, respirator or face mask, visual protection and medical glories. When it is available, it is preferable to use respirators instead of face masks.
Respirers N95 are approved for industrial environments, but the FDA has authorized masks for use under Emergency Use Authorization (U.S).
They’ve been designed to protect against airborne particulates like dust, but their effectiveness against a specific biological agent is not guaranteed for undescribed uses.
When masks are unusable, the CDC recommend the use of Facial Shields or, as a last resort, homemade Masses.
Most COVID--19 cases aren't serious enough to require mechanical or alternative ventilation, but a percentage of cases are.
The type of respiratory support for individuals with COVID-19-related respiratory inadequacy is being studied for people in hospitals, with some evidence that intubation can be avoided with a nasal cannual of high flux or positive airway pressure at two levels.
Whether either of these leads to the same benefits for people who are seriously ill is not known.
Some clinicians prefer to have slurped mechanical ventilation when it’s available because this technique limits aerosol particles to spread, compared to high-race nasal cannula. Severe cases are more common in the elderly (those over 60 and especially over the age of 80).
Many Developing countries do not have enough per capita hospital beds, which limits the capacity of the health system to cope with the sudden escalation in the number of COVID-19 cases serious enough to require hospitalization.
One study in China observed that 5 percent were admitted to intensive care units, 2.3 percent required mechanical ventilation support and 1.4 percent died.
In China, about 30 per cent of people in hospital with COVID-19 were eventually taken to the ICU.
Mechantical ventilation becomes more complex as acute respiratory distress syndrome (ARSD) develops in COVID-19 and oxygenation becomes increasingly difficult.
ventilators who have pressure control mode and high PEEP levels are necessary to increase oxygen delivery while minimizing the risk of lung and pneumothorax lesions associated with ventilation.
High PEEP level might not be available in older ventilators.
