It can also lead to post-intensive syndrome after recovery.
Until March 2020, it was not known if past infections would give effective and long-lasting immunity to people recovering from the disease.
Immunity is seen as likely, based on the behavior of other Coronaviruses, but cases have been reported in which COVID-19 recovery has been followed by tests positive for coronavirus at a later date.
These cases are thought to be worsening of prolonged infection rather than reininfection.
It's believed that the virus is natural and has an animal origin through overpemped infection.
The actual origin is unknown, but as of December 2019, the spread of the infection has become almost entirely driven by human-to-human transmission.
A study of the first 41-confirmed COVID-19 cases, published in January 2020 in The Lancet, has revealed that the earliest date of the onset of symptoms was Dec. 1, 2019.
WHO official publications have reported the older date of the onset of symptoms such as December 8, 2019.
A number of measures are commonly used to quantify mortality.
These numbers vary by region and over time and are influenced by the volume of tests, quality of the health system, treatment options, time elapsed from the initial outbursement, and population characteristics such as age, sex and overall health.
At the end of 2019, the WHO assigned ICD-10 aergarde ICD-10 disease code for deaths resulting from SARS-CoV-2 infection confirmed in the laboratory and U07.2 for COVID-19 deaths with clinical or epidemiological diagnosis, without SARS-CoV-Confectation confirmed in the laboratory.
Based on Johns-Fs Hopkins University statistics, the overall mortality rate was 6.9% (153,822/2,240,191) on April 17, 2020.
Other measures include the mortality rate by cases (CFR), which reflects the percentage of diagnosed individuals who die from one-disinfective disease, and the mortality rate per infection (IFR), which reflects the percentage of infected individuals (diagnosed and undiagnosed and undiagnosed).
These stats are not limited in time and accompany a specific population from the infection to the resolution of the case.
