The impact of a pandemic and its mortality rate are different for both men and women.
Mortality is higher among men in studies conducted in China and in Italy.
The biggest risk for men is in their 50s, with the gap between men and women approaching only at age 90.
In China the death rate was 2.8 per cent for men and 1.7 per cent for women.
Exact ratios for the difference between the sexes aren’t known, but genetic and behavioural factors may be the motive.
Immunological differences due to sex, lower prevalence of female smokers and the development by men of comorbidities such as hypertension at a younger age than women may have contributed to the higher mortality among men.
Fifty-7% of the infected individuals were men, and 72% of those killed by COVID-19 were men.
As of April 2020, the U.S. government wasn’t tracking data for sex-related COVID-19 infections.
Research has been shown that viral diseases such as Ebola, HIV, influenza and SARS affect men and women differently.
A higher percent of health workers, especially nurses, are women, and they are more likely to be exposed to the virus.
The World Health Organization announced on February 11, 2020 that the official name of the disease was “COVID-19”.
WHO chief Tedros Adhanon Ghebreyesus explained that "CO" is "corona", "VI", "virus", "D", disease and "19" when the outbreak has been identified: December 31, 2019.
The naming has been chosen to avoid references to a specific geographical location (e.g. China), animal species or group of people, in accordance with international recommendations of nomenclature to prevent stigmatization. The virus that causes COVID-19 has been referred to as coronavirus with severe acute respiratory syndrome 2 (SARS-CoV-2).
WHO also uses “COVID-19 viruses” and “the COVID-19 virus” in communications to the public.
Both disease and the virus are commonly referred to as "coronavirus".
During the initial outbreak in Wuhan, China, the virus and the disease were commonly referred to as “coronavirus” and “Wuhan coronavirus”.
In January 2020, the WHO recommended "2019-nCov" and "acute respiratory disease by 2019-nCoV" as interim names for the Virus and the Siege, in accordance with 2015 Guidance Against the Use of Locations in Disease Names and Viruse.
Official names COVID-19 and SARS-CoV-2 were published on February 11, 2020.
Due to capacity constraints in standard supply chains, some digital manufacturers are imprinting health material such as nasopharyngeal zarogagotas and respirators.
In one example, when an Italian hospital urgently needed a respirator valve and the vendor was unable to deliver to the necessary schedule, a local startup reverse engineered and imbued the necessary 100 valves overnight.
Following the initial outbreak of COVID-19 arose conspiracy theories, misinformation and misinfect information regarding the origin, size, prevention, treatment and other aspects of the disease, which quickly spread over the Internet.
It appears that humans are capable of transmitting the virus to some other animals.
The study couldn't find evidence of viral replication on pigs, ducks and hens.
No medications or vaccines have been approved for the treatment of the disease.
Religary surveys of COVID-19 Vaccines and medicines are underway in government organizations, academic groups, and industry researchers.
In March, the World Health Organization began "SOLIDARITY Trial" to evaluate the effects of treatment with four existing antiviral components with more likely effectiveness.
There’s no vaccination available, but a number of agencies are actively plunging vaccine.
Previous works on SARS-CoV are being used because both SARS-CoV and SARS-CoV-CoV-2 use ACE2 receiver to enter human cells.
There are three vaccination strategies being investigated.
First, the researchers aim to devise a full-flood vaccine.
The use of this virus, whether inactive or dead, aims to elicit a rapid immunological response of the human body to a re-infection of COVID-19.
A second sub-unit-vaccine strategy aims to create a vaccine that sensitizes the immune system to certain subunits.
For SARS-CoV-CoV-2, this research focuses on the spiculin protein that helps the virus to enter the ACE2 enzyme receiver.
One-third strategy is nucleic Acid (DNA Vaccinations or RNA, a New Technique for Creating Vaccines).
Experimental vaccinations from any of these strategies were supposed to be tested. On March 16, 2020, the first clinical trial of a vaccine began with four volunteers in Seattle.
Vaccination contains an uncontained co-copied genetic code of the virus that causes the disease. Antibodi-redepenting enhancement has been identified as a possible challenge for the development of a vaccine for SARS-CoV-2, but this is controversial.
More than 300 clinical trials have been underway since April 2020.
Seven tests have evaluated treatments already approved for malaria, including four studies on hydroxychlorochloroquine or chloroquine.
Anti-viral drug retargement composes most of China’s surveys, with nine clinical trials in Phase III remdesivir in several countries due to a late-April report.
A synoptic analysis of the clinical development of drug and vaccine candidates for COVID-19 was underway in April 2020. Several existing antiviral drugs are being evaluated to treat COVID-19, including remdesivir, chloroquine and Hydroxychloroquine, lopinavir/ritonavir and lopinavir/ritonavir combined with interferon beta.
There has been an attempt to prove remdesivir's effectiveness since March 2020.
Clinical improvement was observed in remdesivir-treated patients due to compliant.
Phase III Clinical Trial is being carried out in the U.S., China and Italia. Chlolorouquine, already used to treat malaria, has been studied in China in February 2020.
However, there are requests for peer review of the survey.
The Korean and Chinese health officials recommend using chrolochloroquine.
However, Wuhan’s Institute of Virology, although it recommends a daily dose of one turf, warns that double this dose is highly dangerous and can be lethal.
On March 28, 2020, the FDA published an emergency use authorization of hydro hydroxychloroquine and chloroquine at the discretion of physicians who treat COVID-19. The 7th edition of Chinese guidelines also includes interferon, ribavirin or umkernovir for use against COVID-19.
Pre-despite data indicate that high-dose ribavirin is needed to inhibit SARS-CoV-2 in vitro.
